(window.SITE_PARTS=window.SITE_PARTS||{})["bill-integration"]={"plain":{"bills":{"integration":{"slug":"integration","title":"The One Medicare Act","sections":[{"anchor":"sec-0-1","heading":"\u00a7 0-1. Short title","part":null,"body":"This Act may be cited as The One Medicare Act."},{"anchor":"sec-0-2","heading":"\u00a7 0-2. Findings","part":null,"body":"The Congress finds the following:\n\n**(1)** A person aged 64 with income at 150 percent of the poverty level pays little or nothing toward marketplace coverage after the premium tax credit. On turning 65 that person ceases to be eligible for the credit and becomes liable for the full Part B premium.\n\n**(2)** Assistance with that premium is available through the Medicare Savings Programs, which are administered by the States through Medicaid, are subject to an asset test, and require an application. **Fewer than one-third of eligible beneficiaries are enrolled, and as many as 6,000,000 eligible people are not.**\n\n**(3)** A substitute for the asset test based on investment income would permit approximately 78 percent of currently eligible individuals to qualify without one. Several States have removed the asset test or raised its limits.\n\n**(4)** Medicare has an income-related premium **increase** and no comparable income-related **reduction**. The Affordable Care Act has the reverse. **The two do not meet, and the discontinuity falls at 65 on the people with the least.**\n\n**(5)** The income-related monthly adjustment amount operates in brackets. **A single dollar of additional income triggers the full adjustment for the year.** It is computed from income reported two years earlier.\n\n**(6)** A schedule stated as a percentage of income, applied to whatever premium is owed in each period, has no discontinuity at any age or income.\n\n**(7)** What this Act does to the people it reaches is stated here as plainly as the operative text provides it: **no person loses coverage.** Every individual covered by Medicaid on the day before the transition holds Medicare the day after, automatically, with no gap, no application, and no act required (\u00a7 21-1(a), \u00a7 24-2); every individual already entitled to Medicare keeps that entitlement with the guarantee that no change made by this Act ever costs them a dollar more than the prior law would have charged (\u00a7 17-2(e)); every person with employer coverage keeps it, and holds free dormant coverage behind it for the day the job ends (\u00a7 20-2), except that the Federal Government's own employee plan closes, its people move to this program with the Government paying their contribution, and none of them pays more than that plan charged them, adjusted for inflation (\u00a7 21-4(e)). A service outside the uniform program continues at Federal expense through a stated window, and thereafter as the State elects (\u00a7 21-1(c)). **A program's name retires; its people are promoted.**"},{"anchor":"sec-1-1","heading":"\u00a7 1-1. The enhanced premium tax credit, made permanent","part":null,"body":"**(a) NO ELIGIBILITY CLIFF.** \u2014 The applicable percentage under section 36B of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 is determined without regard to whether household income exceeds 400 percent of the poverty level.\n\n**(b) CONTRIBUTION CAP.** \u2014 The required contribution shall not exceed 8.5 percent of household income, and shall be zero for household income not exceeding 150 percent of the poverty level.\n\n**(b-1) CONTINUOUS, AND THE TABLE IT REPLACES IS SUPERSEDED.** \u2014 Between 150 percent of the poverty level and the income at which the cap in subsection (b) is reached, the applicable percentage **rises continuously and without any step, beginning at zero.** **The applicable percentage table under section 36B(b)(3)(A) of that Code, as in effect on the date of enactment, does not apply.**\n\n**(c) PERMANENT.** \u2014 Subsections (a) and (b) apply without expiration.\n\n**(d) WITHIN THE SCHEDULE.** \u2014 The percentages stated in this section are the marketplace portion of the schedule established by section 17-1, and are administered as part of it."},{"anchor":"sec-2-1","heading":"\u00a7 2-1. Medicare buy-in at 55","part":null,"body":"**(a) ELIGIBILITY.** \u2014 An individual who has attained 55 years of age and is not otherwise entitled to benefits under part A may enroll in benefits under parts A, B and D on payment of a premium.\n\n**(b) VOLUNTARY AND ADDITIVE.** \u2014 Enrollment under this section is at the individual's election. **No individual is enrolled, transferred, or made ineligible for any other coverage by reason of this section.**\n\n**(c) PREMIUM.** \u2014 The premium is the actuarial cost of the benefits, and is reduced by the schedule under section 17-1.\n\n**(d) RATING.** \u2014 The premium may vary by age and by rating area, on the same terms permitted for coverage offered on an Exchange.\n\n**(e) TOBACCO USE IS NOT A RATING FACTOR.** \u2014 The premium may not vary by tobacco use.\n\n**(f) PROVIDER PAYMENT.** \u2014 Items and services furnished to an individual enrolled under this section are paid at the rates applicable under title XVIII.\n\n**(g) NO EFFECT ON EMPLOYER COVERAGE.** \u2014 Nothing in this section requires any employer to offer, or to cease offering, coverage."},{"anchor":"sec-7-9","heading":"\u00a7 7-9. Enrollment through the tax return","part":null,"body":"**(a) THE CHECKBOX.** \u2014 The Secretary of the Treasury shall include on the individual income tax return, and on any simplified return, a checkbox by which the taxpayer may authorize disclosure of the information described in subsection (c) to the Exchange serving the taxpayer's residence and to the State agency administering the State plan under title XIX.\n\n**(b) AUTHORITY.** \u2014 Marking the checkbox is a consent to disclosure for purposes of section 6103(c) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, and section 6103 is amended to so provide. **The consent is the whole of the authority, and no disclosure under this section may be made without it.** A disclosure not authorized by this section is subject to sections 7213, 7213A and 7431 of that Code to the same extent as any other unauthorized disclosure.\n\n**(c) WHAT IS DISCLOSED.** \u2014 Only the taxpayer's name, address, household size, household income, and the names and ages of individuals in the household. **No other item of return information may be disclosed under this section, and the omission of an item from this subsection is a determination by Congress and not a gap.**\n\n**(d) DUTY TO ACT, SCREENED.** \u2014 On receipt, and **where the household income disclosed is below the highest income at which any premium assistance is available**, the Exchange or State agency shall determine the individual's eligibility for medical assistance, for enrollment in a qualified health plan, and for any premium assistance, and shall notify the individual of the result, of the plans available, and of the amount payable by the individual.\n\n**(e) ENROLLMENT REQUIRES THE INDIVIDUAL'S ASSENT.** \u2014 No individual may be enrolled in any plan under this section except on the individual's affirmative election after the notice required by subsection (d). **A failure to respond is not an election.**\n\n**(f) SPECIAL ENROLLMENT PERIOD.** \u2014 Authorization under subsection (a) opens a special enrollment period of the length set in the schedule, beginning on the date of the notice under subsection (d).\n\n**(g) THE INFORMATION MAY ONLY HELP.** \u2014 Information disclosed under this section may not be used, by any person, to determine any tax liability, to select a return for examination, to establish or enforce any obligation of the taxpayer other than enrollment elected under subsection (e), or for any purpose relating to the enforcement of the immigration laws. **This subsection may not be waived, and applies notwithstanding any other provision of law.**\n\n**(h) THE RETURN IS COMPLETE EITHER WAY.** \u2014 A return on which the checkbox is not marked is complete, and the failure to mark it is not an omission, a deficiency, or a ground for any consequence."},{"anchor":"sec-7-10","heading":"\u00a7 7-10. Twelve-month continuous eligibility","part":null,"body":"**(b) EXTENSION.** \u2014 Section 1902(e)(12) of the Social Security Act is amended to apply to every individual eligible for medical assistance, without regard to age.\n\n**(c) THE GROUNDS FOR ENDING COVERAGE WITHIN THE PERIOD, AND THEY ARE ALL OF THEM.** \u2014 Medical assistance may be terminated during a 12-month period of continuous eligibility only if \u2014\n\n**(1)** the individual dies;\n\n**(2)** the individual ceases to be a resident of the State;\n\n**(3)** the individual requests termination; or\n\n**(4)** eligibility was obtained by fraud, established as such after notice and an opportunity to respond.\n\n**No other ground, including a change in income, household composition, or employment, may terminate medical assistance during the period. The omission of a ground from this subsection is a determination by Congress and not a gap.**\n\n**(d) REDETERMINATION FREQUENCY.** \u2014 Eligibility may not be redetermined more frequently than once every 12 months. **Section 8-1(e) repeals the contrary requirement.**\n\n**(e) RULE OF CONSTRUCTION.** \u2014 Nothing in this section prevents a State from providing a longer period of continuous eligibility."},{"anchor":"sec-17-1","heading":"\u00a7 17-1. One contribution schedule","part":null,"body":"**(a) ESTABLISHED.** \u2014 There is established a single schedule stating, for each level of household income, the percentage of income an individual is required to contribute toward the cost of coverage.\n\n**(a-1) THE FLOOR.** \u2014 **The required contribution is zero, for premiums and for cost sharing alike, for household income not exceeding the floor set in the schedule to this Act.**\n\n**(b) APPLICATION.** \u2014 The schedule applies to \u2014\n\n**(1)** **the premium for coverage under this title;**\n\n**(2)** **where an individual elects a private plan option under the Public Option Act, the contribution toward the benchmark under section 49(a) of that Act \u2014 and the amount, if any, by which the elected plan's bid exceeds the benchmark is payable by the individual in addition, is not assistance-bearing, and is not part of the schedule;**\n\n**(3)** the premiums for parts B and D of title XVIII;\n\n**(4)** the premium for coverage purchased under section 2-1, **until the general enrollment provisions of this title take effect, whereupon that section's function is absorbed by paragraph (1)**; and\n\n**(5)** the premium for coverage purchased on an Exchange, **for plan years before the transition date stated in \u00a7 24-1, and not thereafter**.\n\n**(c) NO DISCONTINUITY.** \u2014 Except as \u00a7 20-1(a-2) provides, the percentage applicable to an individual does not change by reason of the individual attaining any age, becoming entitled to benefits under title XVIII, or changing the coverage to which the schedule is applied. Only the premium to which the percentage is applied changes.\n\n**(d) CONTINUOUS.** \u2014 The schedule shall be continuous in income. No increment of income may produce a change in the required contribution greater than that increment. **This subsection applies to every amount this section governs \u2014 premium contributions, cost sharing, and the reductions under subsection (f) and \u00a7 17-4 \u2014 and to every schedule value set under this title.**\n\n**(e) UPPER REACH.** \u2014 **The schedule extends continuously from the floor under subsection (a-1) to the income at which the contribution equals the full premium for the coverage elected, and states no upper eligibility limit. An individual whose contribution equals the full premium is enrolled on the same terms as every other individual.**\n\n**(f) COST-SHARING, AND IT PHASES.** \u2014 Cost sharing **under this title** shall be reduced for an individual whose household income does not exceed the level set in the schedule. The reduction phases out continuously across the band set in the schedule, and does not end at a threshold.\n\n**(g) OUT-OF-POCKET PROTECTION SCALES WITH INCOME.** \u2014 **The schedule states, for each level of household income up to the level set in the schedule, an annual out-of-pocket limit, above which cost sharing under this title is zero for the remainder of the year. The limit rises continuously with income and states no value above the band's upper end.** **Protection is income-scaled and fading; there is no universal cap; private supplementation above the band is unimpaired.**"},{"anchor":"sec-17-2","heading":"\u00a7 17-2. Repeals and conversions","part":null,"body":"**(a) MEDICARE SAVINGS PROGRAMS.** \u2014 The qualified Medicare beneficiary, specified low-income Medicare beneficiary and qualifying individual eligibility groups are repealed, and assistance is provided under section 17-1.\n\n**(b) LOW-INCOME SUBSIDY.** \u2014 The premium and cost-sharing subsidy for low-income individuals under part D is repealed, and assistance is provided under section 17-1.\n\n**(c) NO ASSET TEST.** \u2014 Eligibility for assistance under section 17-1 is determined without regard to resources.\n\n**(d) NO APPLICATION.** \u2014 Assistance under section 17-1 is applied automatically on the basis of income determined under section 17-3. **No individual is required to apply for it**, except that an individual who is not a child, as \u00a7 20-1(a) defines the term, makes the request under \u00a7 20-1(a-3) as that section provides.\n\n**(e) NO REDUCTION ON TRANSITION \u2014 AND IT IS PERSONAL AND PERMANENT.** \u2014 No individual receiving assistance under a program repealed by this section, **and no individual entitled to benefits under title XVIII on the day before this title's transition date,** and no individual enrolled in medical assistance under title XIX or in child health assistance or pregnancy-related assistance under title XXI of the Social Security Act on the day before that date, shall **at any time** pay more in premiums and cost sharing under this title than that individual would have paid under the provisions of law in effect on the day before that date, applied to the same coverage and income. **The comparison is made automatically, the lesser amount governs, and no election or application is required.**\n\n**(e-1) THE COMPARISON, COMPUTED.** \u2014 The Center publishes the method of the comparison under subsection (e) with the data sufficient to reconstruct it. The comparison is computed without regard to resources, and a condition of the provisions of law it applies that keys on resources is treated as satisfied. Where more than one computation is available under that subsection, the one yielding the lower amount for the individual governs. The Center furnishes each individual to whom subsection (e) applies an annual statement showing both amounts, not later than the period set in the schedule after the close of the reconciliation period under \u00a7 17-3; the statement may be part of any other notice the Center sends the individual.\n\n**(e-2) UNTIL THE COMPARISON IS APPLIED.** \u2014 For each period before the Center applies the comparison under subsection (e) to an individual to whom that subsection applies, the individual's premiums and cost sharing under this title do not exceed those that applied to the individual on the day before the transition date, and a later application of the comparison does not increase any amount for that period. Where the records the Center holds do not show an amount that applied to the individual on that day, the amount is taken to be zero. The Center applies the comparison to an individual for a period when the amounts it charges the individual for that period are computed under subsections (e) and (e-1), as shown in the records from which the Center computes the individual's contribution and cost sharing.\n\n**(f) THE INCOME-RELATED ADJUSTMENT BECOMES A PHASE.** \u2014 The income-related monthly adjustment amount under sections 1839(i) and 1860D-13(a)(7) of the Social Security Act is amended so that the adjustment increases continuously with income. **No bracket, step or threshold may cause an increment of income to produce a larger increase in the adjustment than that increment.** The adjustment under this subsection is a value of the schedule under \u00a7 17-1 for purposes of \u00a7 17-5, and \u00a7 17-5(c) applies to its income bands, each restated as the percentage of the poverty level equal, on the date of enactment of this Act, to the dollar amount it replaces as in effect for the calendar year that includes that date under paragraphs (2), (3), and (5) of section 1839(i) of the Social Security Act (and, for a joint return, as clause (ii) of paragraph (3)(C) of that section applies it), using the poverty level for a household of one for a band stated for an individual return and of two for a band stated for a joint return.\n\n**(g) CURRENT INCOME.** \u2014 The adjustment under subsection (f), and the assistance under section 17-1, are determined from income for the current period, reconciled after the close of the period, and not from income reported for a period two years earlier."},{"anchor":"sec-17-3","heading":"\u00a7 17-3. Income determination","part":null,"body":"**(a) SOURCE.** \u2014 Income for purposes of this section is determined from the income and household determination furnished under the Keep Your Benefits Act.\n\n**(b) STANDING DIRECTION.** \u2014 An individual with coverage to which section 17-1 applies may at any time give a single direction authorizing periodic furnishing of that determination for the purpose of administering this section. **The direction is given once, is revocable, and covers the period of coverage.**\n\n**(c) ATTESTATION IS A CO-EQUAL ROUTE, NOT AN EXCEPTION.** \u2014 An individual may attest to current household income at any time. An attestation of a decrease takes effect immediately and is reconciled after the close of the period.\n\n**(d) ONE-WAY WITHIN THE PERIOD \u2014 AND NOTHING IS RECOVERED.** \u2014 Within a period, a determination or attestation may increase the assistance for which an individual qualifies. It may not decrease it. Decreases take effect **prospectively** at reconciliation after the close of the period, **and apply to future assistance only. No amount of assistance provided under this title is recoverable from the individual, by offset, addition to tax, or otherwise, by reason of a change in income or of any error not the individual's fraud.**\n\n**(e) FALLBACK.** \u2014 If no determination is available under subsection (a), an individual's attestation governs until one is, and assistance is provided on that basis.\n\n**(f) THE DEFAULT IS AUTOMATIC.** \u2014 **For an individual with no determination and no attestation, assistance is set from the most recent return information available under section 6103(l)(23) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, as added by \u00a7 21-4(f), and the individual owes nothing further for the period by reason of that default.** For an individual with none of a determination, an attestation, or return information, household income is taken not to exceed the floor under \u00a7 17-1(a-1) until one is available, and no contribution arises for any period so determined."},{"anchor":"sec-17-4","heading":"\u00a7 17-4. Assistance deepens with incurred costs","part":null,"body":"**(a) THE RULE.** \u2014 Where the cost sharing an individual has incurred under this title within a year exceeds the share of household income set in the schedule, the individual's position on the schedule for the remainder of the year is redetermined as though household income were reduced by the excess. **The redetermination operates automatically, deepens assistance only, and requires no application, filing, or documentation from the individual.**\n\n**(b) COMPUTED FROM WHAT THE PROGRAM ALREADY SEES.** \u2014 The computation under subsection (a) is made from claims and cost-sharing records held under this title. **No receipt, bill, or record may be required from the individual, and the absence of a record the program should hold is resolved in the individual's favor.**\n\n**(c) ONE WAY.** \u2014 A redetermination under this section deepens assistance and never reduces it. It expires with the year, and no consequence of it survives into any later determination.\n\n**(d) PLAN RECORDS FEED THE COMPUTATION.** \u2014 **A private plan option under the Public Option Act shall report to the Secretary, in the form and at the intervals the Public Option Act provides, the cost sharing each enrollee has incurred**, and a report so made satisfies this subsection. Until the Public Option Act provides them, the report is made for each calendar month, not later than the period set in the schedule after its close; a report stating each enrollee's identifier and the cost sharing the enrollee incurred in the month satisfies this subsection whether or not a form is published; and a period in this subsection is that month. A report is made on the earlier of the dates shown by the Secretary's records and by the plan's transmission record, which is conclusive of that date except on fraud. **Where a plan fails to report, the individual's cost sharing is taken to be the maximum the plan's terms permit, and the redetermination is made on that basis.** A report that omits an enrollee is a failure to report as to that enrollee for that period. The assistance attributable to a default under this subsection is the whole of the assistance provided under this section to the individual for each period for which the plan failed to report, whatever the individual's cost sharing in fact was, computed from the records from which the Center computes the individual's contribution and cost sharing, which are conclusive of it except on a showing that the plan reported for the period or that the individual was not enrolled in the plan for the period; it is borne by the plan, and the Administrator sets it off against the program's payments to the plan and, to the extent none remains due, recovers it as a claim of the United States. For purposes of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, an amount set off under this subsection is treated as received by the plan from the program and paid by the plan to the United States. The amount is due by operation of this subsection whether or not a contract states it, and no rule of the law of contracts respecting penalties or liquidated damages applies to it. If the amount is held an excessive fine as applied, the amount for that application is the excess of the assistance provided over the assistance that the cost sharing shown by a report the plan makes for the period after the default, not exceeding what the plan's terms state for the items and services in its encounter records for the period, would have yielded or, absent such a report, the whole; the remainder of this subsection is unaffected."},{"anchor":"sec-17-5","heading":"\u00a7 17-5. General provisions of the schedule","part":null,"body":"**(a) ONE SCHEDULE, STATED ONCE.** \u2014 The schedule established by \u00a7 17-1 is the only instrument by which income affects what any individual pays under this title. **No premium, cost-sharing amount, adjustment, or reduction under this title may be keyed to income except through the schedule**; this subsection does not affect any law outside this title except as \u00a7 21 expressly provides.\n\n**(b) NO CLIFFS, ANYWHERE, AS A RULE OF LAW.** \u2014 No provision of this title, and no schedule value set under it, may cause an increment of income to reduce the sum of an individual's assistance by more than that increment. **A value, rule, or computation that would do so applies instead at the nearest level at which it does not.**\n\n**(c) INDEXING.** \u2014 Every dollar amount in the schedule is indexed annually by the index the schedule names, and until it names one, by the Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers; every percentage and every income band is stated as a percentage of the poverty level and moves with it. **The floor under \u00a7 17-1(a-1) may be raised by the schedule and may not be lowered except by an Act of Congress amending this subsection by express reference.**\n\n**(d) NO CONDITION BUT INCOME.** \u2014 Assistance under the schedule is conditioned on income and on nothing else, except as \u00a7 20-1(a-2) provides. **No requirement of work, reporting, premium payment as a condition of coverage, or conduct of any kind may be attached to it by any authority, and no demonstration or waiver authority under this title reaches this subsection.** Nonpayment of a required contribution is collected as a debt and never as a loss, suspension, or reduction of coverage.\n\n**(d-1) CHARACTER OF ASSISTANCE.** \u2014 **Assistance under the schedule \u2014 a reduced premium, reduced cost sharing, a redetermination under \u00a7 17-4, the protection of \u00a7 17-2(e) and (e-2), and an amount provided under \u00a7 20-1(a-3)(4), including as \u00a7 20-1(a-3)(6)(D)(iii) applies it \u2014 is not includible in gross income for purposes of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, is not a payment subject to information reporting, and is disregarded in administering every Federal and federally assisted program.** This subsection applies to assistance and reductions a State purchases under \u00a7 22-2(a)(1) or (a)(3) as to assistance under the schedule.\n\n**(d-2) EXTINGUISHMENT.** \u2014 A debt under subsection (d) for a period is extinguished where household income for that period, as later determined, did not exceed the floor under \u00a7 17-1(a-1). An extinguishment under this subsection, and any other discharge of a debt under subsection (d), in whole or in part and for any reason, is not income from the discharge of indebtedness for purposes of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 and is not a discharge of indebtedness for purposes of section 6050P of that Code.\n\n**(e) SEVERABILITY WITHIN THE TITLE.** \u2014 Each section of this Title, and each application of the schedule to a coverage type under \u00a7 17-1(b), is severable. A holding respecting one application leaves every other in force."},{"anchor":"sec-20-1","heading":"\u00a7 20-1. Who is eligible","part":null,"body":"**(a) ELIGIBLE INDIVIDUALS.** \u2014 Every individual who resides in the United States, including each territory of the United States, and who is a citizen or national of the United States, is lawfully present, or is a child, is eligible for coverage under this title. In this section, an individual is a child through December 31 of the calendar year in which the individual attains 18 years of age. **Residence in a territory is residence on identical terms, and no provision of this title distinguishes a territory from a State.**\n\n**(a-1) EVERY OTHER RESIDENT MAY ELECT.** \u2014 An individual who resides in the United States, including each territory of the United States, and is not described in subsection (a) may elect coverage under this title at any time. An election is made by any statement of the individual, in any form, to the Center, its contractor, or a provider, and takes effect when made. From the date of the election the individual is an eligible individual, and every provision of this title applies to the individual as to any other eligible individual, except as subsection (a-2) and \u00a7 21-1(c)(4) provide; a declination under \u00a7 20-4 ends the election, and its revocation restores it. An individual eligible under subsection (a) only by reason of being a child who ceases to be a child remains eligible, as though the individual had made the election on the January 1 on which the individual ceased to be a child.\n\n**(a-2) AN ADULT'S INCOME-BASED ASSISTANCE FOLLOWS A STATUS CHECK THE ADULT REQUESTS.** \u2014 For an individual who is not a child, for each month for which no check under subsection (a-3) has established, as paragraphs (4) and (6) of that subsection provide, that the individual is a citizen or national of the United States or is lawfully present, other than a month that includes a day on which the individual is pregnant or a day of the 60-day period beginning on the last day of a pregnancy \u2014\n\n**(1)** the required contribution under \u00a7 17-1 is the full premium for the coverage elected, and \u00a7 17-1(a-1), \u00a7 17-3(f), and \u00a7 17-5(d-2) do not reduce or extinguish it; the second sentence of \u00a7 17-5(d-2) applies to any discharge of it;\n\n**(2)** cost sharing is not reduced under \u00a7 17-1(a-1), (f), or (g) or under \u00a7 17-4; and\n\n**(3)** \u00a7 17-2(e) and (e-2) do not apply, except to emergency services under subsection (c).\n\nThis subsection does not apply to the extent a State purchases the assistance it withholds under \u00a7 22-2(a)(1), and does not apply to a premium under \u00a7 17-1(b)(5) or to the credit under section 36B of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986. For an individual treated under subsection (a-3)(1) as having made a request on the transition date, this subsection applies only for months beginning after the check is complete without establishing the individual's status, or for which subsection (a-3)(6) no longer establishes it, and \u00a7 17-2(e) and (e-2) apply until then. The individual's statement that the individual is pregnant establishes the pregnancy. No individual is required to show citizenship, nationality, or immigration status to obtain coverage under this title, or to obtain assistance under it except through the check under subsection (a-3).\n\n**(a-3) THE STATUS CHECK, MADE ONLY ON REQUEST.** \u2014\n\n**(1) THE REQUEST.** \u2014 An individual who is not a child, or who will cease to be a child at the close of the calendar year, may request the check under this subsection at any time, by any statement, in any form, to the Center, its contractor, or a provider, that the individual seeks the assistance subsection (a-2) withholds. A request made to a provider or contractor is made to the Center when made; the provider or contractor transmits it to the Center not later than the period set in the schedule, and its record of the date is conclusive of that date except on fraud. **No check is made of an individual who has not requested it**, and the absence of a request establishes nothing about any individual's status. An individual to whom \u00a7 17-2(e) applies, other than one enrolled under title XIX of the Social Security Act on the day before the transition date only for care and services under section 1903(v)(2) of that Act, is treated as having made a request on the transition date.\n\n**(2) THE CHECK.** \u2014 On a request, the Center determines whether the individual is a citizen or national of the United States or is lawfully present, by submitting the individual's name and any social security number the individual has to the Commissioner of Social Security for comparison with the Commissioner's records in the manner section 1902(ee)(1)(A) of the Social Security Act provides for a State; and, for an individual who states that the individual is not a citizen or national, or whose citizenship or nationality that comparison does not confirm or for whom no comparison can be made, by submitting to the Secretary of Homeland Security the individual's name, date of birth, any identifying information with respect to the individual's immigration status that the individual provides, and any statement the individual makes of citizenship, nationality, or immigration status, for a determination whether that information is consistent with the records of the Secretary of Homeland Security, in the manner section 1411(c)(2)(B)(i) of the Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act provides. Each section named in the preceding sentence applies as in effect on the date of introduction of this Act, with the Center in place of the State and of the Secretary of Health and Human Services, and whether or not the individual has made an attestation. Section 552a of title 5, United States Code, applies to each comparison and verification under this paragraph and each notice under paragraph (6)(D)(i), each disclosure this subsection requires is made notwithstanding section 552a(b) of that title, and \u00a7 20-9 does not bar an agreement, verification, or notice that section requires; the Administrator and the Commissioner of Social Security, and the Administrator and the Secretary of Homeland Security, shall each have any agreement that section requires, and any agreement the Secretary of Homeland Security requires as a condition of a verification under this paragraph, in effect not later than the period that \u00a7 21-4(h-1)(4) sets for the exchanges there named, and renew it, or enter into a successor agreement, so that an agreement is in effect at all times thereafter, as a ministerial duty owed to each individual whose request under paragraph (1) is pending. Where a comparison or verification under this paragraph, or a notice under paragraph (6)(D)(i), is a matching program, it is one specifically required by statute for purposes of section 552a(u)(4)(C) of that title; the Data Integrity Board of each agency shall approve each such agreement that states what section 552a(o)(1) of that title requires, in time for it to take effect as this paragraph requires, and section 552a(u)(4)(A) and the second sentence of section 552a(u)(4)(C) of that title do not apply to it; each successor agreement shall be transmitted under section 552a(o)(2)(A) of that title not later than 30 days before the agreement it succeeds expires; and, notwithstanding section 552a(o)(2)(C) and (D) of that title, an agreement under this paragraph that expires or is terminated before a successor takes effect remains in effect until one does, and no term of the agreement ends it sooner. The Commissioner of Social Security and the Secretary of Homeland Security each respond to a comparison or verification under this paragraph not later than the period set in the schedule after receiving it, as a ministerial duty owed to the individual who made the request, and the period under paragraph (3) does not begin until the response is received. A comparison or verification is received on the date the Center's transmission record shows, which is conclusive of that date except on fraud. A reply that refers a comparison or verification for further verification is not a response; on receiving one, the Center requests the further verification, which is a verification under this paragraph. A reply that the submission lacks information needed for the verification is a response that does not establish the status. Where the Commissioner or the Secretary withholds a response under section 552a(q)(1) of title 5, United States Code, the Commissioner or the Secretary states in writing each requirement it has reason to believe is not met and the facts on which that belief rests, and publishes the statement in the Federal Register when made, as a ministerial duty, omitting any information that identifies an individual. A verification of the individual's citizenship, nationality, or immigration status already made under Federal law for purposes of title XVIII, XIX, or XXI of that Act or of section 1411 of the Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act, and shown in records the Center holds or obtains, completes the check without more, unless those records show that the status it verified ended before the date of the request. Before sending a notice under paragraph (3), the Center requests each such verification that a State agency or an Exchange holds, and pays the reasonable cost of producing it.\n\n**(3) NOTICE AND A CHANCE TO RESPOND.** \u2014 Where the comparison or verification under paragraph (2) does not establish the individual's status, the Center first seeks to identify and correct any typographical or other clerical error in, or omission of, the name, social security number, or identifying information with respect to immigration status submitted, including by contacting the individual, and resubmits any corrected information; where the status is still not established, the Center notifies the individual, states what the records showed, what subsection (a-2) and paragraph (4) provide if the period under this paragraph ends without the evidence it describes, and that the check may be requested again at any time, and gives the individual not less than 90 days from receipt of the notice, which is presumed to occur 5 days after it is sent unless the individual shows a later date, to present satisfactory documentary evidence of citizenship or nationality, as section 1903(x)(3) of that Act as in effect on the date of introduction of this Act defines it, or evidence that the individual is lawfully present, or to resolve the inconsistency with the Commissioner of Social Security. The check is complete when the evidence or records establish the status, or when that period ends without them. Where a response under paragraph (2) has not been received within the period set in the schedule for it, or cannot be sought because no agreement that section 552a of title 5, United States Code, or the Secretary of Homeland Security as a condition of the verification, requires for it is in effect, or where the check is not complete when the period set in the schedule for its completion has run, the individual may present the evidence this paragraph describes at any time; evidence so presented that establishes the individual's status completes the check, and evidence that does not establish it completes nothing and begins no period under this paragraph. The Center completes the check not later than the period set in the schedule after the request, excluding any day within a period under this paragraph; completion is a ministerial duty, owed to the individual who made the request.\n\n**(4) ASSISTANCE RUNS FROM THE REQUEST AND IS PAID WHEN STATUS IS ESTABLISHED.** \u2014 A check that establishes the individual's status establishes it for each month beginning with the month of the request on which the check was made, but no assistance that subsection (a-2) withholds is provided until the check establishes the status, notwithstanding section 1137(d)(4) or 1902(ee)(1)(B) of that Act or any other provision of law providing a benefit pending verification. For each such month that began before the check established the status, the assistance withheld for the month is provided, not later than the period set in the schedule after the check establishes it, by reducing any contribution the individual has not paid for the month and paying the individual the remainder. Section 17-4(b) applies to computing the assistance withheld as it applies to the computation under \u00a7 17-4(a). The obligation to pay the remainder arises by operation of this paragraph and is not limited to or conditioned on the availability of appropriations, and is not suspended by a lapse in appropriations, a failure to apportion or allot, a deferral, or a proposed rescission; a remainder not paid when due bears interest from the date due until paid at the rate that applies under chapter 39 of title 31, United States Code, to a late payment to a business concern and, with that interest, is recoverable as a claim founded on an Act of Congress. Where a check is not complete when the period set in the schedule for its completion under paragraph (3) has run, then, for each month beginning with the month of the request, the amount by which the full premium under subsection (a-2)(1) exceeds the contribution for which the individual would qualify but for subsection (a-2) is neither billed nor collected, by offset or otherwise, and \u00a7 17-5(d) does not reach it, until the check is complete; where the check then establishes the status, the amount is reduced as this paragraph provides, and where it does not, the amount is due from the day the check is complete, as a debt under \u00a7 17-5(d) and never as a loss, suspension, or reduction of coverage. An amount held under the preceding sentence is not assistance and is not a benefit pending verification. A check that establishes status is made again only on the individual's request. A check that establishes that the individual is lawfully present by reason of a temporary status establishes it for no month after the months paragraph (6) states.\n\n**(5) THE RECORD.** \u2014 A request, a check, and its result are records of eligibility under this title for purposes of \u00a7 20-2(f).\n\n**(6) TEMPORARY LAWFUL PRESENCE IS ESTABLISHED A YEAR AT A TIME.** \u2014\n\n**(A) THE YEAR.** \u2014 Where a check establishes that an individual is lawfully present by reason of a temporary status, and does not establish that the individual is a citizen or national of the United States or is lawfully present by reason of any other status, it establishes the individual's status for the 12 months beginning with the month in which it establishes it or, for an individual who is then a child, with the January 1 on which the individual ceases to be a child, or, where the records or evidence that establish it show the last day of the temporary status and that day falls before the end of those 12 months, through the month that includes that day, and for no later month except as subparagraphs (C) and (D) provide. For the month that includes that last day, the required contribution under \u00a7 17-1 is the contribution for which the individual qualifies, increased by the share of the excess of the full premium for the coverage elected over that contribution that the days of the month after that day bear to all the days of the month, and subsection (a-2)(2) and (3) apply to each item or service furnished after that day, an item or service paid for a stay or other period being furnished on its first day. No check is made, and no record is consulted, during a period under this subparagraph to determine whether the status has ended. A date shown only as a document's expiration, or one that a notice or regulation extends, is not the last day of a temporary status.\n\n**(B) TEMPORARY STATUS.** \u2014 In this subsection, a temporary status is a status, admission, parole, authorization, deferral, or grant by reason of which an individual is lawfully present that, by its terms or by operation of law, is for a period, or ends on a date or on the occurrence of an event, including the completion of a course of study or of employment and the decision of an application, other than by revocation, termination, rescission, abandonment, or removal. Where the check establishes that the individual is lawfully present, and the records or evidence that establish it do not show the status by reason of which the individual is lawfully present, the individual is lawfully present by reason of a temporary status until the verification under paragraph (2), which the Center requests when the check is complete, or evidence the individual presents at any time, shows a status that is not a temporary status; that showing establishes the status, as paragraph (4) provides, for each month beginning with the month of the request on which the check was made. Lawful permanent residence, including on a conditional basis, is not a temporary status.\n\n**(C) THE NOTICE, AND A RENEWAL ASKED FOR IN TIME.** \u2014 Not later than the period set in the schedule before the last month of a period under subparagraph (A), the Center sends the individual, at the last address the Center holds for the individual, one notice stating the last month of the period and, where the period ends with the last day of a temporary status, that day; that the assistance under \u00a7 17-1 and \u00a7 17-4 for which the individual qualifies by income ends after that month or on that day unless the individual requests the check again and the check establishes the individual's status, or, where the period ends with the last day of a temporary status, unless the status is extended as subparagraph (D) provides; how to make the request; and that no request is required to keep coverage. Where the period ends with the twelfth month under subparagraph (A) and the individual requests the check again before the period ends, the individual's status is established for each month after the period that begins before the check on that request is complete. Where that check does not establish the individual's status, subsection (a-2) applies from the first month beginning after it is complete, and no amount of assistance provided for an earlier month is recoverable from the individual, by offset, addition to tax, or otherwise, by reason of the result of the check. Where the period ends with the twelfth month under subparagraph (A) and the Center sends the notice later than this subparagraph requires, or does not send it, the period under subparagraph (A) does not end before the last day of the month in which the period set in the schedule for the notice has run from the date the notice is sent.\n\n**(D) AN EXTENSION, NOTIFIED OR ASKED FOR.** \u2014 Where a period under subparagraph (A) ends with the last day of a temporary status \u2014\n\n**(i)** the Secretary of Homeland Security, not later than the period set in the schedule after granting the individual an extension of that status, or another status by reason of which the individual is lawfully present, or after a notice or regulation extending that status for a class that includes the individual takes effect, notifies the Center of the grant or extension and of the last day, if any, that the records of the Secretary show for the status granted or extended, as a ministerial duty owed to the individual, for each individual whose verification under paragraph (2) showed that last day;\n\n**(ii)** the individual may request the check again at any time, as paragraph (1) provides; and\n\n**(iii)** where the notice under clause (i), or the check on a request under clause (ii), shows that the individual was lawfully present on the day after that last day, the period under subparagraph (A) continues as though the last day of the temporary status were the last day, if any, that the notice or check shows for the status by reason of which the individual is then lawfully present, and not beyond the twelfth month under that subparagraph; the assistance withheld for each month of the continued period that began before the notice is received or the check establishes the status is provided as paragraph (4) provides, with the period set in the schedule for it running from that receipt or establishment; and a check on such a request also establishes the individual's status, for the months after the continued period, as any other check does.\n\nWhere a period under subparagraph (A) ends with the last day of a temporary status and the Center has not received a notice under clause (i) within the period set in the schedule after that day, the Center, at the close of that period, sends the individual, at the last address the Center holds for the individual, one notice stating that the assistance under \u00a7 17-1 and \u00a7 17-4 for which the individual qualifies by income ended on that day; that a check on a request under clause (ii) that shows the individual lawfully present on the day after that day continues the assistance from that day; how to make the request; and that no request is required to keep coverage.\n\n**(a-4) THE NOTICE IN THE YEAR A CHILD TURNS 18.** \u2014 In each calendar year, not later than the period set in the schedule before the annual election period under \u00a7 20-3(b) begins, the Center sends each individual with coverage under this title who attains 18 years of age in that year, at the last address the Center holds for the individual, one notice, identical for every such individual whatever the Center's records show, stating that the assistance under \u00a7 17-1 and \u00a7 17-4 for which the individual qualifies by income ends on the following January 1 unless the individual requests the check under subsection (a-3) and the check establishes that the individual is a citizen or national of the United States or is lawfully present; that the check is made only on request; how to make the request; and that no request is required to keep coverage.\n\n**(b) ENTITLED INDIVIDUALS.** \u2014 An individual entitled to benefits under title XVIII on the day before the transition date remains entitled, and this title adds to that entitlement and never subtracts from it (\u00a7 17-2(e)).\n\n**(c) EMERGENCY CARE FOR OTHERS.** \u2014 An individual not described in subsection (a), whether or not the individual has made the election under subsection (a-1), receives emergency services on the terms section 1903(v) of the Social Security Act provided on the date of introduction of this Act, carried forward under this title as a payment rule, and the election does not reduce what this subsection provides. The terms so carried forward do not include the requirement to demonstrate community engagement under section 1902(xx) of that Act or any other requirement of work or community engagement, whether imposed directly or through section 1903(v)(2)(B) of that Act.\n\n**(d) NO OTHER TEST.** \u2014 Eligibility under this section is determined by this section and by nothing else. No waiting period, durational residence test, enrollment window, or condition of any kind applies to eligibility, and the omission of any other test is a determination of Congress and not a gap. **Sections 401, 402, 403, 411, 421, and 432 of the Personal Responsibility and Work Opportunity Reconciliation Act of 1996 (8 U.S.C. 1611, 1612, 1613, 1621, 1631, and 1642) do not apply to coverage, a benefit, or assistance under this title, or to an election under \u00a7 22-2. Sections 1818(a)(3), 1836(a)(2), and 1899C of the Social Security Act (42 U.S.C. 1395i-2(a)(3), 1395o(a)(2), and 1395mmm) do not apply to coverage, a benefit, or assistance under this title, including where this Act places that coverage within title XVIII of that Act.** No debt accrues under an affidavit of support under section 213A of the Immigration and Nationality Act (8 U.S.C. 1183a) against any sponsor of an individual on the basis of coverage, a benefit, or assistance under this title, and the cost of any of them is not an unreimbursed cost for purposes of that section."},{"anchor":"sec-20-2","heading":"\u00a7 20-2. Coverage exists by operation of law \u2014 active and dormant","part":null,"body":"**(a) THE STATUS.** \u2014 Every eligible individual is covered under this title **by operation of this subsection, from birth or from the moment of becoming eligible, without application, election, enrollment act, or action by any agency or officer.** An agency records coverage; nothing an agency does or fails to do creates, conditions, or ends it.\n\n**(b) ACTIVE AND DORMANT.** \u2014 Coverage is **active** unless dormant. Coverage is **dormant** while the individual is enrolled in qualifying coverage \u2014 coverage under a group health plan meeting the standards of sections 27-1 through 27-6 of the Medical Care PAID Act, or other coverage a provision of this title names \u2014 **and the individual has not elected active coverage.** An election of active coverage is available to every individual at any time, whatever other coverage the individual holds.\n\n**(c) DORMANCY IS FREE AND ENDS ITSELF.** \u2014 Dormant coverage bears no premium and no contribution. **On any loss or lapse of the qualifying coverage, for any reason, coverage under this title is active by operation of this subsection, effective at the moment of the loss, with no act by the individual, no gap, and no notice as a condition**.\n\n**(d) ONLY ACTIVE COVERAGE COSTS.** \u2014 The contribution schedule (\u00a7 17) applies to active coverage only. **No premium, contribution, or debt arises from dormant coverage or from the status itself**.\n\n**(e) WHO PAYS FIRST, AND THE PERSON NEVER PAYS MORE FOR IT.** \u2014\n\n**(1) THE EMPLOYER PLAN PAYS FIRST.** \u2014 Section 1862(b)(1)(A)(i)(I), (b)(2), (b)(3)(A), and (b)(4) of the Social Security Act apply, without regard to age or to the number of employees of any employer, to every individual with active coverage under this title who is covered under a group health plan by virtue of the individual's current employment status with an employer, as though that coverage were entitlement under section 226(a) of that Act and a payment under this title were a payment under title XVIII. An individual with end stage renal disease is within this paragraph on the same terms as any other individual.\n\n**(2) THE INJURY INSURER PAYS FIRST.** \u2014 Section 1862(b)(2), (b)(3)(A), (b)(4), and (b)(9) of that Act apply to every individual with active coverage under this title, as though a payment under this title were a payment under title XVIII, with respect to payment made, or that can reasonably be expected to be made, under a workmen's compensation law or plan of the United States or a State, under an automobile or liability insurance policy or plan (including a self-insured plan), or under no fault insurance. Under this paragraph \u2014\n\n**(A)** the payment that section 1862(b)(2)(B)(i) of that Act authorizes is a duty: the Administrator shall make it for an item or service covered under this title whenever such a primary plan has not made, or cannot reasonably be expected to make, payment for the item or service promptly within the meaning of that clause;\n\n**(B)** recovery under section 1862(b)(2)(B) of that Act runs only against the primary plan and an entity that is or was required or responsible, as clause (iii) of that section describes, to make payment under it, and never against the individual, the individual's estate, or any amount paid to or for the individual by reason of the injury or illness, including the proceeds of a settlement, judgment, or award, nor against any person by reason of having received such an amount; and\n\n**(C)** the 3-year period in the last sentence of section 1862(b)(2)(B)(iii) of that Act runs from the date on which the Center receives notice of the settlement, judgment, award, or other payment, from any source.\n\n**(3) THE PERSON NEVER PAYS MORE.** \u2014 For an item or service covered under this title for which a group health plan pays first under paragraph (1) \u2014\n\n**(A)** the individual owes the lesser of the amount the terms of the group health plan leave the individual owing after the payment by that plan and the payment under this title, and the cost sharing the individual would owe under this title for the item or service, at the individual's position under \u00a7 17-1 and \u00a7 17-4 on the date it is furnished, if the program paid first;\n\n**(B)** the amount under subparagraph (A) is cost sharing under this title for purposes of \u00a7 17-1(g), \u00a7 17-2(e), and \u00a7 17-4, and is incurred toward, and reduced by, each of them as any other cost sharing under this title is; and\n\n**(C)** a provider or supplier that furnishes the item or service and submits a claim for it under this title accepts the payment by the group health plan, the payment under this title, and the amount under subparagraph (A), together, as payment in full, and may not charge or collect any further amount for the item or service from the individual or from any person on the individual's behalf.\n\n**(4) THE EXCISE.** \u2014 For purposes of section 5000 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, a group health plan that at any time during a calendar year does not comply with section 1862(b)(1)(A) or (b)(2) of the Social Security Act as paragraph (1) applies them is a nonconforming group health plan, except that a failure with respect to an individual to whom clause (i) of section 1862(b)(1)(A) of that Act would not apply by reason of clause (ii) of that section, or by reason of clause (iii) of that section determined as though the plan had elected treatment under that clause, is disregarded. Section 5000(d) of that Code applies to this paragraph, and the plan of a governmental entity remains subject to paragraph (1) and to recovery under section 1862(b)(2)(B) of the Social Security Act.\n\n**(5) NO PAYMENT TO LEAVE THE EMPLOYER PLAN.** \u2014 Section 1862(b)(3)(C) of that Act applies to an incentive offered to an individual who has active coverage under this title, or may elect it under \u00a7 20-1(a-1), or would have it on not enrolling in or terminating enrollment under the plan, not to enroll (or to terminate enrollment) under a group health plan under which the individual would be covered by virtue of current employment status with an employer, as though the individual were entitled to benefits under title XVIII and the plan would be a primary plan as defined in section 1862(b)(2)(A) of that Act, whether the individual's coverage under this title would then be active or dormant, except that \u2014\n\n**(A)** a payment that \u00a7 27-4 requires is not an incentive; and\n\n**(B)** a civil money penalty under section 1862(b)(3)(C) of that Act as this paragraph applies it is imposed only by a district court of the United States in a civil action brought by the Attorney General, and section 1128A of that Act does not apply to it.\n\n**(6) THE PLAN REPORTS TO THE CENTER.** \u2014 Section 1862(b)(7)(A) and (D) of that Act apply to a group health plan with respect to every individual covered under the plan who has coverage under this title, as though that coverage were entitlement under title XVIII and the plan were a primary plan to the program under this title, with the information submitted to the Center, except that \u2014\n\n**(A)** this paragraph does not apply with respect to an individual to whom clause (i) of section 1862(b)(1)(A) of that Act would not apply by reason of clause (ii) of that section, or by reason of clause (iii) of that section determined as though the plan had elected treatment under that clause;\n\n**(B)** section 1862(b)(7)(B) and (C) of that Act do not apply, and nothing in this paragraph discloses to any employer or plan sponsor whether any individual's coverage under this title is active or dormant; and\n\n**(C)** where an entity, a plan administrator, or a fiduciary described in section 1862(b)(7)(A) of that Act fails to submit information this paragraph requires with respect to an individual, the group health plan is treated as the primary plan under paragraph (1) for each item or service paid under this title for that individual during the period of the failure, whether or not paragraph (1) otherwise reaches the individual, and the amount so paid is recovered from the plan and from any entity described in section 1862(b)(2)(B)(iii) of that Act under section 1862(b)(2)(B) of that Act as paragraph (1) applies it.\n\n**(7) THE RECOVERY RETURNS TO THE PROGRAM.** \u2014 An amount recovered under section 1862(b)(2)(B) of that Act for a payment under this title is credited to the account from which the payment was made.\n\n**(8) TERMS, AND THE IMPORTED TEXT READ FOR THIS TITLE.** \u2014 In this subsection, \"group health plan\" has the meaning given in section 1862(b)(1)(A)(v) of the Social Security Act, and section 1862(b)(1)(D) and (E) of that Act apply, including to the count of employees under clauses (ii) and (iii) of section 1862(b)(1)(A) of that Act for purposes of paragraphs (4) and (6). In each provision of section 1862(b) of that Act as this subsection applies it, a reference to the appropriate Trust Fund is a reference to the account from which the payment under this title was made; a reference to deductibles and coinsurance under title XVIII is a reference to cost sharing under this title; a reference to the program established under title XVIII is a reference to the program; and a reference to items and services furnished under title XVIII is a reference to items and services furnished under this title.\n\n**(f) THE RECORDS MAY ONLY HELP.** \u2014 No record of coverage, eligibility, attestation, or claims under this title or under title XIX or XXI of the Social Security Act, whoever holds it, and no information derived from such a record, may be disclosed or used, by any person, for any purpose relating to the enforcement of the immigration laws, or to reduce or end any benefit of, or to establish or enforce any obligation of, any individual, except in administering this title and its companion Acts. These bars apply notwithstanding any other provision of law, including any provision authorizing disclosure between agencies, section 290(b) of the Immigration and Nationality Act (8 U.S.C. 1360(b)), section 642 of the Illegal Immigration Reform and Immigrant Responsibility Act of 1996 (8 U.S.C. 1373), and section 434 of the Personal Responsibility and Work Opportunity Reconciliation Act of 1996 (8 U.S.C. 1644), and apply to any other authority invoked to obtain or use such a record or information for substantially the same purpose. The Center publishes annually each disclosure of such records outside the Department of Health and Human Services, with the authority under which it was made, in a form that cannot be associated with, or otherwise identify, directly or indirectly, a particular individual to whom a record relates or, as to return information, a particular taxpayer. Each private plan option under the Public Option Act, and each contractor of the Center, shall report to the Center each disclosure it makes of such a record or information to any person other than the Department of Health and Human Services, stating the date of the disclosure, the person to whom it was made or, where that person is a natural person, the class of person, the number of individuals whose records or information it disclosed, and the authority under which it was made, and identifying no individual, not later than the period set in the schedule after the disclosure, and the Center includes each disclosure so reported in that publication; disclosures to the same person under the same authority within a month may be reported as one entry stating their number. A disclosure whose existence an order of a court of the United States forbids revealing is reported, and published, not later than the period set in the schedule after the order expires. No other order or requirement of law, including an order of a court of a State and a law of a State or of a political subdivision of a State, defers, excuses, or forbids a report under this subsection.\n\n**(g) CHARACTER OF COVERAGE.** \u2014\n\n**(1) NOT INCOME.** \u2014 For purposes of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, none of the following is includible in the gross income of the individual to whom it relates, or of any other individual by reason of the coverage of the individual to whom it relates \u2014\n\n**(A)** the value of an individual's coverage under this title, active or dormant;\n\n**(B)** the value of each item or service furnished to an individual under this title, as a benefit of the program under an Act named in \u00a7 0-6(a), under a function continued by \u00a7 21-1(c), or under an election by a State under \u00a7 22-2;\n\n**(C)** each payment the Administrator makes, under this title (including under subsection (e)), for a benefit of the program under an Act named in \u00a7 0-6(a), under a function continued by \u00a7 21-1(c), or under such an election, to any person for an item or service so furnished to an individual, and each payment the Administrator makes under \u00a7 21-1(c)(9); and\n\n**(D)** each reduction in an individual's contribution or cost sharing that a State purchases under \u00a7 22-2(a)(1) or (a)(3).\n\n**(2) NO DOUBLE BENEFIT.** \u2014 Paragraph (1) does not apply to an amount attributable to (and not in excess of) a deduction allowed under section 162(l) or 213 of that Code for any prior taxable year.\n\n**(3) WHAT THIS SUBSECTION DOES NOT REACH.** \u2014 This subsection does not affect the gross income of any person from a payment for an item or service that person furnishes, or the application of section 131 of that Code to such a payment; the treatment under that Code of a payment by any person other than the Administrator or a State paying for an election under \u00a7 22-2, or of an amount any person other than such a State pays toward an individual's contribution under \u00a7 17; or any return or statement required by section 6055 or 6056 of that Code."},{"anchor":"sec-20-3","heading":"\u00a7 20-3. The default, and the election among options","part":null,"body":"**(a) THE DEFAULT.** \u2014 An individual with active coverage who has made no election is enrolled in the public plan. **No assignment process, algorithm, or discretion exists** \u2014 the default is the public plan for everyone, always.\n\n**(b) ELECTION.** \u2014 An individual may elect a private plan option under the Public Option Act, or return to the public plan, during the annual election period and on any qualifying change in circumstances. **An election changes the option; it never touches the coverage status.**\n\n**(c) ONLY THE INDIVIDUAL ELECTS, AND IS TOLD OF EVERY CHANGE.** \u2014 An election takes effect only when made by the individual or the individual's authorized representative. The Center notifies the individual directly of every change of option, not later than the period set in the schedule after the change. An individual who states that a change was not the individual's election is restored to the prior option as of the date of the change, and every amount paid to a plan for that period is recovered from the plan, never from the individual; the Administrator recovers it by set-off against the program's payments to the plan. Any amount the individual paid toward the plan for that period is credited against the individual's contribution under the prior option for that period, and the excess is refunded to the individual."},{"anchor":"sec-20-4","heading":"\u00a7 20-4. Declination \u2014 and no penalty, ever","part":null,"body":"**(a)** An individual may decline active coverage by a written declination, effective prospectively; a declination is revocable at any time, and revocation is effective immediately on receipt.\n\n**(b) DECLINING COSTS ONLY THE COVERAGE.** \u2014 No late-enrollment penalty, premium surcharge, waiting period, or adverse consequence of any kind attaches to a declination, its duration, or its revocation. **The late enrollment increases under sections 1839(b) and 1860D-13(b) of the Social Security Act do not apply to any individual, and are repealed by \u00a7 21**."},{"anchor":"sec-20-5","heading":"\u00a7 20-5. Birth, and the card","part":null,"body":"**(a) COVERED FROM BIRTH.** \u2014 An individual born in the United States or a territory is covered from birth under \u00a7 20-2(a). **This subsection states what that section already provides, so that no rule of any program, plan, or State may put it in doubt.**\n\n**(b) THE CARD RIDES EXISTING MACHINERY.** \u2014 Documentation of coverage is issued through the enumeration-at-birth process jointly administered with the Commissioner of Social Security, as part of birth registration, as a ministerial duty. **The card does not display the social security number**, creates no new identity document or registry, and **its absence has no effect on coverage or on any person's duties under this title.**"},{"anchor":"sec-20-6","heading":"\u00a7 20-6. Point-of-care enrollment in the records","part":null,"body":"**(a)** Where an individual's coverage is not reflected in the records available to a provider, the individual's attestation of identity and residence enrolls the individual in the records at the point of care, and the provider **may rely on the attestation, is held harmless in that reliance, and is paid** for covered items and services furnished in reliance on it.\n\n**(b)** A subsequent determination affects future records only, per \u00a7 17-3(d)'s one-way rule; **no payment to a provider under subsection (a) is recouped except on the provider's fraud.**\n\n**(c) RELIANCE, WATCHED.** \u2014 The Center reviews claims paid in reliance on an attestation under subsection (a) after payment, publishes annually their number and amount by provider, and refers to the Inspector General of the Department of Health and Human Services each provider for which the share of its claims paid in the year that were paid in reliance on such an attestation exceeds that share among providers enrolled under the same provider type or specialty, in the same geographic unit set in the schedule, by more than the margin set in the schedule. Review under this subsection affects no individual's coverage and no payment except on the provider's fraud."},{"anchor":"sec-20-7","heading":"\u00a7 20-7. Officials are covered like everyone else","part":null,"body":"**(a)** Each Member of Congress, the President, the Vice President, each officer serving in a position at levels I through V of the Executive Schedule, and each justice and judge of a court established under Article III, including a justice or judge retired from regular active service who continues to hold office, **is covered under this title on the same terms, the same schedule, and with the same elections \u2014 including the private option \u2014 as any other individual**, and chapter 89 of title 5, United States Code, does not apply to such an individual.\n\n**(b)** This section is severable from every other provision of this title, and its invalidity as to any office affects no other office and no other section.\n\n**(c) SITTING JUSTICES AND JUDGES.** \u2014 A justice or judge holding office on the day before this section first applies to that justice or judge shall at no time pay more in premiums and cost sharing under this title, for the justice or judge and every individual who would have been covered with the justice or judge under chapter 89 of title 5, United States Code, than the amount section 8906 of that title would have required to be withheld from the pay of the justice or judge, and the cost sharing the terms of the contract under section 8902 of that title would have required, for enrollment covering those individuals in the plan in which the justice or judge was then enrolled, each as determined for the contract year concerned or, for a contract year in which that plan is not offered under that chapter, in whichever plan then offered under that chapter yields the lower amount; and, for a contract year beginning on or after the transition date, each as \u00a7 21-4(e)(4) would determine it for the justice or judge, whether or not that paragraph otherwise applies, from the last contract year that began before this section first applied to the justice or judge. In this subsection, premiums under this title include the premiums under \u00a7 17-1(b)(3) and the adjustment under \u00a7 17-2(f). The cost sharing the terms of a contract would have required is the cost sharing those terms state for the item or service when furnished by a provider in the plan's network with every referral and authorization those terms require, as shown in the statement of benefits published for the plan for the contract year concerned. Where those terms state a limit on annual out-of-pocket cost sharing, the cost sharing those terms would have required for those individuals for the items and services furnished to them in a contract year does not exceed that limit. The comparison is made automatically, the lesser amount governs, no election or application is required, and the difference is borne by the program. The difference borne by the program is not includible in the gross income of the justice or judge or of any other individual, and is not wages for purposes of chapters 21 and 24 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986. This subsection applies for so long as the justice or judge holds that office."},{"anchor":"sec-20-8","heading":"\u00a7 20-8. Waiting periods are abolished","part":null,"body":"The 24-month waiting period for benefits on the basis of disability under section 226(b) of the Social Security Act is repealed, and **no provision of law conditions coverage under this title on the passage of time.** The precise conforming amendments are made by \u00a7 21."},{"anchor":"sec-20-9","heading":"\u00a7 20-9. Closure","part":null,"body":"Coverage, activity, default, election, declination, and documentation under this Title operate as this Title provides and on no other condition. **No agency may add a process, requirement, verification step, or condition to any of them by regulation, guidance, instruction, or agreement; every duty of an agency under this Title is ministerial; and the omission of a process from this Title is a determination of Congress and not a gap.** The check under \u00a7 20-1(a-3) is the only verification of citizenship, nationality, or immigration status under this Title, and is made only on the individual's request."},{"anchor":"sec-21-1","heading":"\u00a7 21-1. Repeal, and the rule against silent extinguishment","part":null,"body":"**(a) REPEAL.** \u2014 Titles XIX and XXI of the Social Security Act are repealed, effective on the transition date, and the coverage they provided is succeeded by this title as \u00a7 20-2 provides \u2014 **no individual's coverage ends, changes form by even a day's gap, or requires any act, by reason of the repeal.**\n\n**(b) THE SUCCESSION TABLE GOVERNS WHAT IT NAMES.** \u2014 Each function listed in \u00a7 21-2 is succeeded as there provided.\n\n**(c) NOTHING DIES SILENTLY \u2014 WHAT CONTINUES, CONTINUES FEDERAL, FOR A STATED TIME.** \u2014\n\n**(1) THE CONTINUATION PERIOD.** \u2014 A function performed under title XIX or XXI on the day before the transition date and not addressed by \u00a7 21-2 continues in effect under this title as a Federal function, administered by the Administrator of the Center for Medicare Services within the machinery of this title, with every payment obligation assumed by the United States, through the continuation period set in the schedule for the State in which the function was performed or, for a function not performed in or for a particular State, through the continuation period of the State whose period closes last. It ends at the close of that period, or earlier on the effective date of an Act of Congress enacted after this Act that addresses it by name. An item or service that a State plan under title XIX or XXI covered on the day before the transition date, and that the program does not cover on the transition date, is a function not addressed by \u00a7 21-2 and continues under this paragraph, whatever row of \u00a7 21-2 describes the individuals it served. The ending under this paragraph is an express rule of this Act that displaces contrary law, for purposes of \u00a7 21-5.\n\n**(2) THE BRIDGES THE TABLE NAMES.** \u2014 A function that \u00a7 21-2 routes to continuation under this subsection continues as paragraph (1) provides, except that it continues until the successor \u00a7 21-2 names for it takes effect, and the continuation period does not apply to it. Section 0-6(b) and (c) do not apply to this paragraph. The ending under this paragraph is an express rule of this Act that displaces contrary law, for purposes of \u00a7 21-5. Where a function is described both by a row of \u00a7 21-2 naming its substance and by the row for \u00a7 1115 demonstrations, the row naming its substance governs.\n\n**(3) THE TERMS.** \u2014 A function continues under this subsection on the terms in effect on the date of introduction of this Act, without regard to any amendment or approval submitted or granted after that date, and including any formula in those terms that adjusts an amount over time. An increase in a function's scope, amount, duration, or payment rate made after that date, and a function first performed after that date, continues only as a State elects and pays under \u00a7 22-2. Where \u00a7 21-2 states the terms on which a function continues, those terms govern. Section 1108 of the Social Security Act does not limit a function continued under this subsection.\n\n**(4) WHAT DOES NOT CONTINUE.** \u2014 This subsection does not continue coverage of, or payment for, an individual not described in \u00a7 20-1(a) (emergency services are provided by \u00a7 20-1(c), not under this subsection), or any function of a State, or expenditure by a State, for which no Federal financial participation was available on the day before the transition date, determined without regard to any limitation under section 1108 of the Social Security Act. Such coverage, function, or expenditure continues only as a State elects and pays under \u00a7 22-2.\n\n**(5) AFTER THE PERIOD.** \u2014 A State may continue a function described in paragraph (1) beyond the close of its continuation period, on the terms paragraph (3) states or on broader terms, by an election under \u00a7 22-2(a)(4). An election in force at the close of the period continues the function without interruption. A course of treatment begun under a function continued under paragraph (1) before the close of its continuation period is completed under it, and the United States bears its cost.\n\n**(6) THE LIST, PUBLISHED.** \u2014 Not later than the period set in the schedule after the transition date, the Center publishes, for each State, each function continued under paragraph (1), the date on which its continuation period ends, the number of individuals it served in the most recent year for which the Center holds records, and the price under \u00a7 22-2(c) of continuing it. Publication is a ministerial duty. A function omitted from the publication continues under paragraph (1) all the same.\n\n**(7) NO SILENT EXTINGUISHMENT.** \u2014 The repeal in subsection (a) extinguishes no duty, payment, right, or program except as this Title provides expressly, and the omission of a function from \u00a7 21-2 is a direction to continue it under this subsection, not a gap. Continuation under this subsection imposes no duty on any State (\u00a7 21-1A).\n\n**(8) PAYMENTS ALREADY EARNED.** \u2014 Payment to a State for an expenditure made before the transition date continues under the law in effect on the day before that date until settled, and paragraphs (1) and (3) do not apply to it.\n\n**(9) CLAIMS ALREADY INCURRED.** \u2014 An amount owed under a State plan under title XIX or XXI, or under a contract entered into under such a plan, for an item or service furnished before the transition date and unpaid on that date is paid by the Administrator at the expense of the United States, on the terms of that plan or contract in effect on the date the item or service was furnished, including its terms for the time within which a claim must be submitted. Paragraphs (1) and (3) do not apply to a payment under this paragraph, and no State is liable for it (\u00a7 21-1A(a)).\n\n**(10) PACE.** \u2014\n\n**(A) THE TWO HALVES.** \u2014 The terms under section 1934 of the Social Security Act of each PACE program agreement in effect on the date of introduction of this Act are a function to which paragraph (1) applies as a function not addressed by \u00a7 21-2, for purposes of paragraphs (1), (5), and (6) only. The terms of that agreement under section 1894 of that Act continue under title XVIII on the terms paragraph (3) states, for as long as its terms under section 1934 continue under this subsection or under an election under \u00a7 22-2(a)(4), and end when those terms end. Each agreement is one agreement as to both. The ending under this subparagraph is an express rule of this Act that displaces contrary law, for purposes of \u00a7 21-5.\n\n**(B) THE STATE'S PLACE.** \u2014 The Administrator acts in the place of the State, and of each agency of the State, in every role that either section, the agreement, or the regulations under either section give them, on the terms on which each role was exercised on the date of introduction of this Act.\n\n**(C) THE REFERENTS.** \u2014 For each such agreement, a reference in either section, in the agreement, or in those regulations to the State plan, to medical assistance, to eligibility for medical assistance, or to items and services covered under title XIX is a reference to the State plan of the State concerned, and to eligibility and coverage under it, as in effect on the date of introduction of this Act, determined by the Administrator. Section 21-3(a) does not apply to any such reference.\n\n**(D) THE RATES.** \u2014 For a contract year beginning on or after the transition date \u2014\n\n**(i)** the capitation amount under section 1934(d) of that Act is the amount the agreement specified for the contract year that included the date of introduction of this Act, adjusted by any formula the agreement then stated; where that formula computes from amounts paid under a State plan, it computes from those amounts for the most recent year in which they were paid; and\n\n**(ii)** the capitation amount under section 1894(d) of that Act is computed, under the methodology the agreement stated on the date of introduction of this Act, from the annual capitation rates the Secretary announces for the year under section 1853(b)(1)(B)(i) of that Act, whether or not any organization is paid at those rates, and is paid from the Trust Funds that section 1853(f) of that Act names. Section 49 of the Public Option Act does not apply to it, and the appropriation in \u00a7 21-5 bears only the terms under section 1934.\n\n**(E) ONE PAYER FOR WHAT THE AGREEMENT COVERS.** \u2014 While an individual is enrolled under such an agreement, the individual receives the items and services the agreement's terms require the PACE provider to furnish solely through the PACE program, and section 1894(a)(1)(B)(i) of that Act applies only to those items and services. No payment is made under this title for any of them furnished outside the program except as the agreement provides. Every other benefit of this title, and of title XVIII, is furnished to the individual as to any other covered individual.\n\n**(F) THE YEARLY TERMS ARE NOT NEW AGREEMENTS.** \u2014 The extension of such an agreement for a contract year, the specification of a contract year's capitation amount under subparagraph (D), and an act of the Administrator under subparagraph (B) are not the entry of an agreement for purposes of \u00a7 22-4(b).\n\n**(G) AFTER THE PERIOD.** \u2014 For an election under paragraph (5) continuing the terms under section 1934, the payments under section 1894 are not expended for the election and are not part of its price under \u00a7 22-2(c) or paragraph (6), and \u00a7 22-1(b) does not apply to the ending of the terms under section 1894 under subparagraph (A). The completion of a course of treatment under paragraph (5) carries the items and services of the course, furnished by the providers furnishing them, at the expense of the United States, and not the individual's enrollment in the PACE program or any capitation amount.\n\n**(H) NO NEW AGREEMENT.** \u2014 No PACE program agreement under section 1894 or 1934 of the Social Security Act is entered on or after the date of introduction of this Act, except by an Act of Congress enacted after this Act that addresses PACE by name."},{"anchor":"sec-21-1a","heading":"\u00a7 21-1A. The States are released, and the agency is renamed","part":null,"body":"**(a) EVERY STATE DUTY ENDS.** \u2014 On the transition date, every duty, obligation, financial contribution, plan requirement, reporting requirement, and administrative role of a State or political subdivision under title XIX or XXI, or with respect to any function this Title continues, ends. **No provision of this title imposes any duty on a State**, except an obligation a State assumes by its own election through a route under subsection (c) or \u00a7 24-3(b), and the omission of any State duty is a determination of Congress and not a gap.\n\n**(b) A STATE MAY CLOSE ITS MEDICAID OFFICE.** \u2014 A State may retain, restructure, or **abolish entirely** its agency administering the former State plan, at its sole choice, on its own schedule, and no consequence under Federal law attaches to the choice. **Nothing in this title requires a State to maintain any office, system, workforce, or appropriation.**\n\n**(c) STATE INVOLVEMENT IS VOLUNTARY, AND THESE ARE THE ROUTES.** \u2014 A State participates in the program only by its own election, through \u2014 **(1)** a paid transition contract under \u00a7 24; **(2)** the supplementation lane of \u00a7 22, at the State's sole expense; or **(3)** a services agreement in the nature of an agreement under section 1864 of the Social Security Act, paid and terminable. **The routes stated in this subsection are the routes**, and no other State role exists to be commandeered, conditioned, or withheld. **This subsection closes State roles in administering the program; it does not touch a State's exercise of its own regulatory authority over providers, insurance, or public health** \u2014 including a hospital rate system qualified under section 7-13 of the Keeping Hospitals Open Act, whose rates this title honors as title XVIII has honored Maryland's since 1977.\n\n**(d) THE AGENCY RENAMED.** \u2014 The Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services is redesignated the **Center for Medicare Services**; and a reference in any law, regulation, or document to the former designation, or to the Administrator thereof, is deemed a reference to the redesignated agency and its Administrator."},{"anchor":"sec-21-2","heading":"\u00a7 21-2. The succession table","part":null,"body":"| Function under titles XIX/XXI | Succession |\n|---|---|\n| Coverage of the eligible population | **\u00a7 20-2** (by operation of law; active/dormant) |\n| EPSDT (\u00a7 1905(r)) | **the Medicare Covers Teeth, Eyes, and Ears Act's pediatric standard** \u2014 carried verbatim for everyone under 21; until that Act's effective date, continued per \u00a7 21-1(c) |\n| Long-term services and supports; HCBS waivers; institutional care | **the Long-Term Care Act** \u2014 and until its effective date, continued per \u00a7 21-1(c) with no waiting-list expansion frozen |\n| Drug rebates (\u00a7 1927) | **Continued, applied to drugs under this title, until the pricing provisions of the Lowest Drug Price Wins Act first bind**; \u00a7 0-6(b) and (c) do not apply to this row |\n| MSPs and cost-sharing protection | **\u00a7 17-2(a)** |\n| CHIP (title XXI) | **\u00a7 20-2** \u2014 children are covered individuals; the separate program ends |\n| **Vaccines for Children (\u00a7 1928)** | **Named expressly**: the purchase and free-administration machinery continues under the Medicare Covers Teeth, Eyes, and Ears Act's pediatric standard, uninterrupted |\n| DSH (\u00a7 1923) and safety-net supplements | **the Keeping Hospitals Open Act \u00a7 6-7's measured payer-mix payment** |\n| NEMT (\u00a7 1902(a)(4)/regulatory duty) | **the Medicare Covers Teeth, Eyes, and Ears Act's scheduled benefit**; until then, \u00a7 21-1(c) |\n| School-based services; TCM | **\u00a7 21-1(c) continuation**, then the Medicare Covers Teeth, Eyes, and Ears Act |\n| IHS and tribal payment provisions (100% FMAP class) | **\u00a7 21-1(c) continuation on no-less-favorable terms**, pending the Tribal Health Act |\n| PACE (\u00a7 1934), and each PACE program agreement's terms under \u00a7 1894 | **\u00a7 21-1(c)(10)** \u2014 continued through the continuation period, and after that period as a State elects under \u00a7 21-1(c)(5); \u00a7 21-1(c)(2) does not apply to this row, and neither the long-term-care row nor the managed-care row reaches a program agreement |\n| Managed-care contracts under the former titles, capitation included | **\u00a7 24-4(b)** \u2014 not a function continued under \u00a7 21-1(c); this row does not address a payment the State directed under such a contract to a provider |\n| State eligibility workforces and systems | **\u00a7 24 transition contracts** \u2014 States as paid contractors |\n| \u00a7 1115 demonstrations | **\u00a7 22's conversion: expand-only, 100% State-funded** |\n| Emergency services for individuals outside \u00a7 20-1(a) (\u00a7 1903(v)) | **\u00a7 20-1(c)**, without limit of time |\n| Estate recovery (\u00a7 1917) | **Ends with the repeal, expressly** |\n| Provider taxes as State-share financing | **Moot** \u2014 there is no State share; no successor |"},{"anchor":"sec-21-3","heading":"\u00a7 21-3. References translate, and the sweep is owed","part":null,"body":"**(a) THE DEEMING RULE.** \u2014 On and after the transition date, a reference in any Federal law to title XIX or XXI of the Social Security Act, to a State plan under either, to medical assistance, or to eligibility under either, **is deemed a reference to this title and to coverage under it**, except where \u00a7 21-2 routes the function elsewhere, in which case the reference follows the function. A reference deemed under this subsection does not make a condition of, limitation on, or requirement to verify eligibility under the law containing it, including section 1137(a)(1) and (d) of the Social Security Act (42 U.S.C. 1320b-7(a)(1), (d)) and section 402(b) of the Personal Responsibility and Work Opportunity Reconciliation Act of 1996 (8 U.S.C. 1612(b)), a condition of coverage, a benefit, or assistance under this title; \u00a7 20-1 alone governs eligibility.\n\n**(b) THE ENUMERATED CONFORMING AMENDMENTS.** \u2014 *(Reserved.)*"},{"anchor":"sec-21-4","heading":"\u00a7 21-4. The retirements \u2014 each made coherent by this Act, none before its replacement binds","part":null,"body":"**(a) THE PREMIUM TAX CREDIT.** \u2014 Section 36B of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 is repealed **effective on the transition date and not before**, and the repeal applies only with respect to months beginning on or after that date. **For each month beginning before that date, section 36B of that Code, as in effect on the day before that date, continues to apply, including the allowance of the credit, on any return for a taxable year that includes the month, whenever the return is filed.**\n\n**(b) LATE-ENROLLMENT PENALTIES.** \u2014 Sections 1839(b) and 1860D-13(b) increases repealed per \u00a7 20-4(b).\n\n**(c) THE 24-MONTH WAIT.** \u2014 Section 226(b) conformed per \u00a7 20-8.\n\n**(d) COBRA.** \u2014 The continuation-coverage provisions \u2014 part 6 of subtitle B of title I of ERISA, section 4980B of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, and the parallel PHSA provisions \u2014 are repealed effective on the transition date. **Transition courtesy: an individual receiving continuation coverage on the transition date may complete the elected period under the plan's terms**, active coverage under \u00a7 20-2 standing behind it throughout; and no qualifying event after the transition date creates a continuation right.\n\n**(e) THE FEDERAL EMPLOYEE HEALTH PLAN CLOSES, AND THE GOVERNMENT PAYS ITS PEOPLE'S CONTRIBUTION.** \u2014\n\n**(1) WHO MOVES.** \u2014 On the transition date, each covered Federal individual who is an eligible individual under \u00a7 20-1 is covered under this title as any other eligible individual, and not under chapter 89 of title 5, United States Code, except as paragraph (2) provides. In this subsection, a \"covered Federal individual\" is an individual described in \u00a7 20-7(a); an employee or annuitant, as section 8901 of that title defines each, an officer or employee of the Postal Service, and a Postal Service annuitant as section 8903c(a) of that title defines the term, who is, or but for this subsection or section 1312(d)(3)(D) of the Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act would be, eligible to enroll or to continue enrollment in a health benefits plan under that chapter, notwithstanding section 2105(e) of that title and section 1005(f) of title 39, United States Code; and each member of family, as that section defines it, of such an employee or annuitant. Chapter 89 continues to apply to a covered Federal individual other than one described in \u00a7 20-7(a) for each period in which the individual is not an eligible individual under \u00a7 20-1.\n\n**(2) BARGAINED COVERAGE RUNS TO THE END OF ITS AGREEMENT.** \u2014 Where a collective-bargaining agreement in effect on the date of enactment of this Act provides for the health benefits coverage of an employee, including by a variation under section 1005(f) of title 39, United States Code, chapter 89 of title 5 continues to apply to the employee and the employee's members of family, while the employee is in a unit the agreement covers, until the agreement expires, and paragraph (1) applies to them on the day after. An agreement expires on the earlier of the date its terms as in effect on the date of enactment state, without regard to any extension, renewal, or continuation after that date by agreement, by operation of a term, by an arbitration award, or by law, and the effective date of an agreement or award that succeeds it. The parties may agree that the coverage ends earlier. **No collective-bargaining agreement entered into, and no arbitration award issued, after the date of enactment of this Act, including one that succeeds, extends, or renews an agreement in effect on that date, may provide for health benefits coverage of an employee of the United States or of the Postal Service other than coverage under this title**, and a term that does is without effect.\n\n**(3) THE GOVERNMENT PAYS THE CONTRIBUTION.** \u2014 For each month in which a covered Federal individual who is an employee or an individual described in \u00a7 20-7(a), or who is an annuitant, is covered under this title by reason of paragraph (1), the required contribution under \u00a7 17-1 for the active coverage under this title of that individual and of each member of family of that individual is paid to the program in full, by the person, and from the appropriations or funds, that would have paid the Government contribution under section 8906 of title 5 for that individual. Each payment under this paragraph is a Government contribution under section 8906 of that title for purposes of subsections (f) and (g) of that section and of section 8909a(b) of that title. The individual owes no part of it. The Center bills each paying office monthly in the aggregate, without identifying any individual's household income, and an amount unpaid when due is recoverable as a claim founded on an Act of Congress. Section 223(b)(8)(B)(i) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 does not apply to an individual who ceases to be an eligible individual under section 223(c)(1) of that Code by reason of paragraph (1) or (5). A payment under this paragraph is not includible in the gross income of any individual, and is not wages for purposes of chapters 21 and 24 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986.\n\n**(4) NO ONE WHO MOVES PAYS MORE.** \u2014 An individual enrolled, or covered as a member of family, under chapter 89 of title 5 on the day before paragraph (1) first applies to the individual shall, for each month in which the individual would, but for this subsection, have been eligible to be so covered, at no time pay more in premiums and cost sharing under this title, for the individual and every individual who would have been covered with the individual under that chapter, than the enrollee share of the subscription charge under section 8906 of that title, and the cost sharing the terms of the contract under section 8902 or 8903c of that title would have required for enrollment covering those individuals in the plan and option in which the individual was then enrolled, each as stated for the last contract year that began before that day, with each dollar amount increased for each later calendar year by the percentage by which the Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers for the 12 months ending with August of the preceding calendar year exceeds that index for the 12 months ending with August of the calendar year in which that last contract year began. Subsections (e-1) and (e-2) of \u00a7 17-2 apply to the comparison under this paragraph as to the comparison under subsection (e) of that section, with the amounts under this paragraph in place of the amounts that applied on the day before the transition date. The Office of Personnel Management and each office that would have paid a Government contribution under section 8906 of title 5 furnish the Center, not later than the period set in the schedule before the transition date and for each month thereafter, the enrollment, plan, option, and employment status of each individual to whom this paragraph or paragraph (3) applies; where the records do not show the plan and option, the comparison uses the plan and option yielding the lowest amount. The cost sharing the terms of a contract would have required is determined as \u00a7 20-7(c) determines it. The comparison is made automatically, the lesser amount governs, no election or application is required, and the difference is borne by the program. The difference borne by the program is not includible in the gross income of any individual, and is not wages for purposes of chapters 21 and 24 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986.\n\n**(5) NO NEW CONTRACTS.** \u2014 Each contract under section 8902 or 8903c of title 5 terminates at the close of the last contract year beginning before the transition date, notwithstanding any term providing for its renewal, and the Office of Personnel Management shall not enter into or renew such a contract for a later contract year, except in each case to cover individuals to whom chapter 89 of that title continues to apply under paragraph (1) or (2).\n\n**(6) THIS SUBSECTION DISPLACES CONTRARY LAW.** \u2014 This subsection applies notwithstanding section 1005(f) and chapter 12 of title 39, United States Code, and chapter 71 of title 5, United States Code, and is an express rule of this Act that displaces contrary law, for purposes of \u00a7 21-5.\n\n**(f) THE RETURN-INFORMATION DISCLOSURE.** \u2014\n\n**(1)** Section 6103(l) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> \"(23) DISCLOSURE OF RETURN INFORMATION TO THE CENTER FOR MEDICARE SERVICES FOR THE CONTRIBUTION SCHEDULE.\u2014\n>\n> \"(A) IN GENERAL.\u2014The Secretary, upon written request from the Center, shall disclose by data exchange to officers, employees, and contractors of the Center return information of any taxpayer whose income is relevant in placing on the schedule established by section 17-1 of the One Medicare Act an individual described in section 17-3(f) of that Act, not later than the period set in the schedule to that Act after the later of the date the request was made and, where section 552a(o) of title 5, United States Code, requires an agreement for the disclosure and none is in effect on that date, the date on which one takes effect, excluding from the period any day on which no agreement that section requires is in effect. Such return information shall be limited to\u2014 (i) taxpayer identity information with respect to such taxpayer; (ii) the filing status of such taxpayer; (iii) the number of individuals for whom a deduction is allowed under section 151 with respect to the taxpayer (including the taxpayer and the taxpayer's spouse); (iv) the items of income shown on the return that are necessary to place the individual on that schedule; and (v) the taxable year to which the preceding information relates or, if applicable, the fact that such information is not available.\n>\n> \"(B) RESTRICTION ON USE.\u2014Return information disclosed under subparagraph (A) may be used by officers, employees, and contractors of the Center only for the purposes of, and to the extent necessary in\u2014 (i) setting assistance under section 17-3(f) of the One Medicare Act and the administration of the schedule established by section 17-1 of that Act; and (ii) compiling statistics that Act requires to be published, which are published only in a form that cannot be associated with, or otherwise identify, directly or indirectly, a particular taxpayer.\n>\n> \"(C) THE INFORMATION MAY ONLY HELP.\u2014Neither return information disclosed under subparagraph (A) nor any record or information derived from it may be used to determine any tax liability, to select any return for examination, to establish or enforce any obligation of the taxpayer or of any other individual to whom it relates, or for any purpose relating to the enforcement of the immigration laws. Section 7701(d) of title 31, United States Code, does not apply to that return information or to any record or information derived from it. A use or disclosure of a record or information derived from return information disclosed under subparagraph (A) in violation of this subparagraph is a disclosure in violation of this section for purposes of section 7431. This subparagraph may not be waived and applies notwithstanding any other provision of law.\n>\n> \"(D) CENTER.\u2014For purposes of this paragraph, the term 'Center' has the meaning given in paragraph (24)(G).\"\n\n**(2)** The Administrator shall make a request under section 6103(l)(23) of that Code for each individual with coverage to which \u00a7 17-1 applies for whom neither a determination under \u00a7 17-3(a) nor an attestation under \u00a7 17-3(c) is available, not later than the period set in the schedule after the individual is first so described, and, while the individual remains so described, again in each calendar year not later than the period set in the schedule after the date prescribed for filing an individual income tax return for the preceding calendar year (determined without regard to any extension).\n\n**(3)** Section 6103(p)(4) of that Code is amended as \u00a7 21-4(h-1)(2) provides, and sections 6103(a)(3) and 7213(a)(2) of that Code as \u00a7 21-4(h-1)(3) provides.\n\n**(4)** This subsection, and the amendment made by it, take effect on the date of enactment of this Act.\n\n**(g) THE EXCHANGE-ERA MACHINERY, WOUND DOWN BY NAME.** \u2014 Each of the following keys on the subsidy structure this Act retires, and each is disposed of expressly rather than left to go silently inert:\n\n**(1)** the cost-sharing reduction machinery of section 1402 of the Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act is spent on the transition date and is repealed effective that date;\n\n**(2)** a Basic Health Program under section 1331 of that Act terminates on the transition date \u2014 its enrollees are covered individuals under \u00a7 20-2 and convert under \u00a7 24-2, its wind-up proceeds under \u00a7 24-4's contract machinery, and no individual's coverage lapses for any reason of the termination;\n\n**(3)** a waiver under section 1332 of that Act expires on the transition date, and to the extent it expands care, \u00a7 22-4(a)'s expand-only conversion applies to it as to a former title XIX waiver;\n\n**(4)** the Exchange functions under section 1311 of that Act end on the transition date; a State may repurpose its exchange as an enrollment and plan-election portal by contract under \u00a7 24-4(a), and records transfer per that section; and\n\n**(5)** section 1411(e)(4)(B)(iii) and (C) of that Act, as each applies to an employer, and section 1411(f)(2) of that Act apply only with respect to an enrollee's eligibility for a premium tax credit or a cost-sharing reduction for a month beginning before the transition date.\n\n**(h) THE EMPLOYER PROVISIONS \u2014 ONE REPEALED IN THE OPEN, TWO KEPT ON PURPOSE.** \u2014 Section 4980H of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 is repealed effective on the transition date: **its assessable payment triggers only on an employee's receipt of a credit this Act retires, so leaving it in place would repeal it silently, and this Act does not repeal things silently.** Whether an employer-payment obligation succeeds it is **reserved expressly to the funding legislation**, with section 27-6(b) of the Medical Care PAID Act \u2014 the omission is a scheduled decision and not a gap. Sections 6055 and 6056 of that Code **continue** (section 6056 as subsection (h-2) amends it), conformed so that the coverage reporting they require feeds the qualifying-coverage status under \u00a7 20-2(b) \u2014 **the reporting exists so dormancy tracks reality, and it survives the credit it once served.**\n\n**(h-1) THE COVERAGE RETURNS REACH THE CENTER.** \u2014\n\n**(1)** Section 6103(l) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> \"(24) DISCLOSURE OF COVERAGE INFORMATION TO THE CENTER FOR MEDICARE SERVICES.\u2014\n>\n> \"(A) IN GENERAL.\u2014The Secretary shall disclose by data exchange to officers, employees, and contractors of the Center the following return information from each return made under section 6055 or 6056, and from each correction of such a return, not later than the period set in the schedule to the One Medicare Act after the latest of the date prescribed for filing the return (determined without regard to any extension), the date on which the Secretary receives the return or correction, and, where section 552a(o) of title 5, United States Code, requires an agreement for the disclosure and none is in effect on the later of those dates, the date on which one takes effect, excluding from the period any day on which no agreement that section requires is in effect: (i) the taxpayer identity of each individual whom the return shows as covered and, where the return shows that individual's date of birth in place of a taxpayer identifying number, that date of birth; (ii) the months or dates of each such individual's coverage that the return shows; (iii) the origin of the coverage that the return shows; (iv) the name and employer identification number of the person making the return and of any employer the return names; and (v) the calendar year to which the information relates.\n>\n> \"(B) RESTRICTION ON USE.\u2014Return information disclosed under subparagraph (A) may be used by officers, employees, and contractors of the Center only for the purposes of, and to the extent necessary in\u2014 (i) determining and recording under section 20-2(b) of the One Medicare Act whether an individual holds qualifying coverage, and giving that record effect in administering that Act, including, before the transition date under section 24-1 of that Act, preparing the classification under section 24-2(3) of that Act; (ii) identifying under section 20-2(e) of the One Medicare Act the group health plan that pays first; (iii) recovering under section 1862(b)(2)(B) of the Social Security Act, as section 20-2(e) of the One Medicare Act applies it, from that plan or from any entity that is or was required or responsible, as clause (iii) of section 1862(b)(2)(B) of the Social Security Act describes, to make payment under that plan; and (iv) compiling statistics the One Medicare Act requires to be published, which are published only in a form that cannot be associated with, or otherwise identify, directly or indirectly, a particular taxpayer.\n>\n> \"(C) DISCLOSURE FOR PRESENTING A CLAIM.\u2014The Center, or a contractor acting for it, may disclose to an insurer or third-party administrator of a group health plan from which recovery may be made under subparagraph (B)(iii) the name and taxpayer identifying number of an individual whom the return shows as covered under the plan (or, where the return shows that individual's date of birth in place of a taxpayer identifying number, that date of birth), and the months of that coverage, only for the purpose of presenting a claim under section 1862(b)(2)(B) of the Social Security Act or of determining whether that coverage was by virtue of the individual's current employment status. An insurer or third-party administrator may use information disclosed to it under this subparagraph or subparagraph (E) only for responding to that claim or determination.\n>\n> \"(D) THE INFORMATION MAY ONLY HELP.\u2014Return information disclosed under this paragraph may not be used to determine any tax liability or to select any return for examination. Neither that return information nor any record or information derived from it may be used to establish or enforce any obligation of any individual in that individual's capacity as a person whom the return shows as covered, or for any purpose relating to the enforcement of the immigration laws, and no amount is recovered from any individual in that capacity by reason of either. Section 7701(d) of title 31, United States Code, does not apply to that return information or to any record or information derived from it. This subparagraph may not be waived and applies notwithstanding any other provision of law.\n>\n> \"(E) DISCLOSURE FOR RECOVERY.\u2014The Center, or a contractor acting for it, may disclose return information disclosed under subparagraph (A) to officers, employees, and contractors of the Department of Justice, of the Department of the Treasury acting on a claim transferred or referred to it under section 3711(g), 3716(c)(6), or 3720A of title 31, United States Code, and of an executive department or agency operating a debt collection center to which such a claim is referred under section 3711(g)(4)(A) of that title, solely for recovering a claim described in subparagraph (B)(iii) or for the defense of any litigation or administrative proceeding arising from such recovery; each of them may use it only for that purpose and may disclose it to another of them only for that purpose. Such return information may be disclosed in a judicial or administrative proceeding to recover such a claim or arising from such recovery, except that any of it identifying an individual whom the return shows as covered (i) is filed under seal without necessity of obtaining a court order, remains under seal, and is accompanied by a copy from which it is redacted for the public record, and (ii) is disclosed in the proceeding to no person other than the court and its officers and employees, the trier of fact, the United States, and an insurer or third-party administrator to which subparagraph (C) permits its disclosure, together with counsel and other persons that insurer or administrator retains for the proceeding.\n>\n> \"(F) A REFUSAL IS STATED AND PUBLISHED.\u2014A determination by the Secretary that the requirements of paragraph (4) or (9) of subsection (p) are not met with respect to the Center or a contractor of the Center for return information under this paragraph, and any refusal to make a disclosure under subparagraph (A), shall be in writing, shall state each requirement not met and the facts on which the determination rests, and shall be published in the Federal Register when made, as a ministerial duty, omitting any return or return information and any fact whose publication would impair the security of returns or return information.\n>\n> \"(G) CENTER.\u2014For purposes of this paragraph, the term 'Center' means the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services or, on and after its redesignation by section 21-1A(d) of the One Medicare Act, the Center for Medicare Services.\"\n\n**(2)** Section 6103(p)(4) of that Code is amended by striking \"(17), or (22)\" each place it appears and inserting \"(17), (22), (23), or (24)\".\n\n**(3)** Section 6103(a)(3) of that Code is amended by striking \"(16), (19), (20), or (21) of subsection (l)\" and inserting \"(16), (19), (20), (21), (23), or (24) of subsection (l)\"; and section 7213(a)(2) of that Code is amended by striking \"(19), (20), or (21) or (m)\" and inserting \"(19), (20), (21), (23), or (24) or (m)\".\n\n**(4)** An exchange under section 6103(l)(23) or (24) of that Code is subject to section 552a of title 5, United States Code, and \u00a7 20-9 does not bar an agreement, verification, or notice that section requires. Where that section requires an agreement for either exchange, the Secretary of the Treasury and the Administrator shall have it in effect not later than the period set in the schedule after the date of enactment of this Act, and shall renew it, or enter into a successor agreement, so that an agreement is in effect at all times thereafter. Where either exchange is a matching program, it is one specifically required by statute for purposes of section 552a(u)(4)(C) of that title. The Data Integrity Board of each agency shall act on each such agreement in time for it to take effect as this paragraph requires, and each successor agreement shall be transmitted under section 552a(o)(2)(A) of that title not later than 30 days before the agreement it succeeds expires.\n\n**(5)** This subsection, and the amendments made by it, take effect on the date of enactment of this Act, and section 6103(l)(24)(A) of that Code, as added by paragraph (1), applies to each return and correction that the Secretary receives on or after that date.\n\n**(h-2) SECTION 6056 RUNS WITHOUT SECTION 4980H, AND THE QSEHRA STATEMENTS END WITH SECTION 36B.** \u2014\n\n**(1)** Section 6056 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 is amended so that subsection (a), clause (i) of subsection (b)(2)(C), and subsection (f) read as follows:\n\n> \"(a) IN GENERAL.\u2014Every applicable large employer ~~required to meet the requirements of section 4980H with respect to its full-time employees during~~ **with respect to** a calendar year shall, at such time as the Secretary may prescribe, make a return described in subsection (b).\"\n\n> \"(i) the length of any waiting period (as defined in section ~~2701(b)(4)~~ **2704(b)(4)** of the Public Health Service Act) with respect to such coverage,\"\n\n> \"(f) DEFINITIONS.\u2014For purposes of this section **and of section 9831(d)(3)(B)**, any term used in this section which is also used in section 4980H ~~shall have~~ **, as in effect on the day before the transition date under section 24-1 of the One Medicare Act, has** the meaning given such term by section 4980H **as so in effect. The regulations issued for purposes of section 4980H (including sections 54.4980H-1 through 54.4980H-6 of title 26, Code of Federal Regulations), as in effect on that day, are ratified as so in effect and continue in effect as regulations for purposes of this section, of section 9831(d)(3)(B), and of the regulations under each, until amended, modified, superseded, or revoked by the Secretary for those purposes in accordance with law; and for those purposes section 4980H(c)(4)(B), as so in effect, continues to apply. For a calendar year beginning before the transition date, each such term and regulation applies as in effect for that calendar year**.\"\n\n**(2)** Section 9831(d)(3)(B)(i) of that Code is amended to read as follows:\n\n> \"(i) is not an applicable large employer as defined in section 4980H(c)(2) **as section 6056(f) applies it**, and\"\n\n**(2A)** Section 9831(d)(4)(B)(ii) of that Code is amended to read as follows:\n\n> \"(ii) A statement that the eligible employee should provide the information described in clause (i) to any health insurance exchange to which the employee applies for advance payment of the premium assistance tax credit. **This clause applies only to a notice that is provided before the transition date under section 24-1 of the One Medicare Act and is for a year beginning before that date.**\"\n\n**(2B)** Section 6051(a)(15) of that Code is amended to read as follows:\n\n> \"(15) **in the case of a statement for a calendar year beginning before the transition date under section 24-1 of the One Medicare Act,** the total amount of permitted benefit (as defined in section 9831(d)(3)(C)) for the year under a qualified small employer health reimbursement arrangement (as defined in section 9831(d)(2)) with respect to the employee,\"\n\n**(3)** The amendments made by paragraphs (1) and (2) take effect on the transition date and apply, on and after that date, to every return and statement under section 6056 of that Code, whatever the calendar year to which it relates, and to every determination under section 9831(d)(3)(B) of that Code, made on or after that date. The amendments made by paragraphs (2A) and (2B) take effect on the date of enactment of this Act; the amendment made by paragraph (2A) applies to every notice under section 9831(d)(4) of that Code provided on or after that date, and the amendment made by paragraph (2B) applies to every statement under section 6051 of that Code furnished on or after that date, whatever the calendar year to which it relates."},{"anchor":"sec-21-5","heading":"\u00a7 21-5. Closure","part":null,"body":"The repeals and retirements made by this Title are those it states expressly, and no repeal by implication arises from this Act. **Where a provision of law can be read to have survived or to have been repealed, it survived** \u2014 the construction that continues a function prevails, and \u00a7 21-1(c) supplies the responsible official and the funds. This presumption yields to any express rule of this Act that displaces contrary law, applies notwithstanding any other provision of law, or states that an omission is a determination of Congress; such a rule governs according to its terms. There are appropriated, without fiscal year limitation, such sums as are necessary for functions continued under \u00a7 21-1(c) and for payments under \u00a7 21-1(c)(8) and (9), to remain available until expended."},{"anchor":"sec-22-1","heading":"\u00a7 22-1. The federal floor is untouchable by this Title","part":null,"body":"**(a)** Coverage, benefits, the schedule, and every protection under this title are identical in every State and territory, except functions continued under \u00a7 21-1(c), **whether or not the State participates in any lane this Title creates, and whatever it purchases or declines.**\n\n**(b)** No Federal payment, benefit, term, approval, or treatment of any kind may be conditioned on a State's participation, payment, or election under this Title, and **no officer may request, encourage, or negotiate a State payment except as this Title provides.** A lane payment purchases the add-on it names and affects nothing else."},{"anchor":"sec-22-2","heading":"\u00a7 22-2. The supplementation lane","part":null,"body":"**(a) WHAT A STATE MAY BUY.** \u2014 A State may elect, by law of the State, to purchase from the program, for its residents, at the State's sole expense \u2014 \n\n**(1)** for its residents to whom \u00a7 20-1(a-2) applies or would apply but for an election under this paragraph, the assistance that subsection withholds, in whole or in part;\n\n**(2)** benefits additional to those under this title and its companion Acts;\n\n**(3)** reductions in its residents' contributions or cost sharing below the schedule; or\n\n**(4)** a function, or an increase in a function, that \u00a7 21-1(c) does not continue or has ceased to continue.\n\n**(b) EXPAND-ONLY.** \u2014 An election under this section may only add coverage, benefits, assistance, or a function under subsection (a)(4). **Nothing a State elects, purchases, or declines may reduce, condition, or delay any individual's coverage, benefit, or assistance under this title**, and an election purporting to is without effect.\n\n**(c) PRICE AND PAYMENT.** \u2014 The price of an election is its actuarial cost to the program or, for a function that is not a benefit, the cost of performing it, computed and published with the data sufficient to reconstruct it; payment flows from the State to the Trust Fund, and amounts so paid are appropriated to the Center for the election, to remain available until expended; **no Federal funds may be expended for an election**, except that an election under subsection (a)(1) takes effect on the date the State's law provides, the Center publishes its price not later than the period set in the schedule after receiving that law, and assistance applied before the State pays is a receivable of the Trust Fund and not an expenditure of Federal funds; and a State's nonpayment ends the election prospectively as subsection (e) provides, never retroactively against any individual.\n\n**(d) ADMINISTERED AS ONE PROGRAM.** \u2014 An election is administered by the Center for Medicare Services within the machinery of this title. To the individual it is invisible: one card, one schedule, one set of rules, with the add-on applied automatically.\n\n**(e) ENDING AN ELECTION.** \u2014 A State may end an election prospectively, on notice of not less than the period set in the schedule, published to the affected residents. **An individual's federal-floor coverage is unaffected by construction; a course of treatment begun under an add-on is completed under it**; and amounts owed for the notice period remain owed.\n\n**(f) THE LEDGER.** \u2014 The Center publishes annually, per State, the elections in force, their prices, their enrollment, and the amounts paid. Publication is a ministerial duty."},{"anchor":"sec-22-3","heading":"\u00a7 22-3. The demonstration authority \u2014 criteria, not deals","part":null,"body":"**(a) THE AUTHORITY.** \u2014 A State, a plan offered under the Public Option Act, or the Center may apply to conduct a demonstration testing an improvement in the quality, efficiency, coordination, or experience of care under this title.\n\n**(b) THE CRITERIA, AND THEY ARE ALL OF THEM.** \u2014 An application is approved if it \u2014\n\n**(1)** states a testable hypothesis and the measures that would confirm or refute it;\n\n**(2)** **reduces, conditions, or delays no individual's coverage, benefit, or assistance** \u2014 a demonstration only adds, and an application proposing otherwise is outside this section;\n\n**(3)** carries an independent evaluation, designed before the demonstration begins, whose results and data are published;\n\n**(4)** ends by its own terms not later than the period set in the schedule, and continues only by a new application; and\n\n**(5)** identifies its cost and its funding, which for a State application is the State's, for a plan application the plan's, and for a Center application the program's, within the amount set in the schedule.\n\n**(c) APPROVAL IS CONDITIONED, NOT DISCRETIONARY.** \u2014 The Center shall approve an application meeting the criteria. A denial shall be in writing, shall identify the criterion not met and the facts on which that determination rests, and shall be published.\n\n**(d) THE UNTOUCHABLES.** \u2014 No demonstration may touch eligibility (\u00a7 20-1), the automatic and permanent character of coverage (\u00a7 20-2), the conditions closure (\u00a7 17-5(d), \u00a7 20-9), the schedule's floor (\u00a7 17-1(a-1)), or provider nondiscrimination under the Public Option Act. **This subsection may not be waived, and no demonstration authority elsewhere in law reaches this title.** Section 1115 of the Social Security Act does not apply to this title.\n\n**(e) WHAT A DEMONSTRATION PROVES, SCALES.** \u2014 Where the published evaluation confirms the hypothesis, the Center reports to Congress the amendment that would generalize it. **Scaling is Congress's act, never the Center's**."},{"anchor":"sec-22-4","heading":"\u00a7 22-4. The existing waiver stock converts","part":null,"body":"**(a)** On the transition date, a demonstration or waiver in effect with respect to the former title XIX or XXI \u2014 \n\n**(1)** **to the extent it expands care**, may be continued by the State's election under \u00a7 22-2, at the State's sole expense, without interruption to any individual; and\n\n**(2)** **to the extent it restricts, conditions, or delays care, terminates by operation of law**, and the individuals it reached hold the federal floor.\n\n**(b)** No new waiver, demonstration, or agreement respecting the former titles may be entered after the date of introduction of this Act.\n\n**(c) TITLE XVIII MODELS.** \u2014 A model or demonstration under section 1115A or other authority of title XVIII in effect on the transition date **continues to the end of its current term on its own conditions**; its renewal, extension, or successor proceeds only under \u00a7 22-3. A State hospital-rate model qualifies instead under section 7-13 of the Keeping Hospitals Open Act, which requires no demonstration \u2014 **a model that has proven itself graduates to the statute; it does not live on as a deal.**"},{"anchor":"sec-22-5","heading":"\u00a7 22-5. Closure","part":null,"body":"The lanes stated in this Title, and \u00a7 21-1A(c)'s routes, are the only forms of State participation in the program; the criteria in \u00a7 22-3(b) are the only criteria; and the omission of any other lane, criterion, or authority is a determination of Congress and not a gap."},{"anchor":"sec-24-1","heading":"\u00a7 24-1. The date, and there is no gate","part":null,"body":"**(a)** The **transition date** is the first day of the second calendar year beginning after enactment. Every provision of this title keyed to the transition date takes effect then, **by operation of law**.\n\n**(b) NO READINESS GATE.** \u2014 No certification, determination, finding of readiness, or systems milestone conditions the transition date, and none may be created by regulation.\n\n**(c) THE TRANSITION PERIOD.** \u2014 The transition period, for \u00a7 24-3(a), \u00a7 24-4(b), \u00a7 24-7, and every other provision of this Act using the term, begins on the transition date and runs for the period set in the schedule."},{"anchor":"sec-24-2","heading":"\u00a7 24-2. Day-one conversion, with nobody filing anything","part":null,"body":"On the transition date, by operation of law and without any act by any individual \u2014\n\n**(1)** each individual enrolled in the former titles XIX or XXI is **actively covered** under \u00a7 20-2;\n\n**(2)** each individual entitled under title XVIII continues without interruption, \u00a7 17-2(e)'s lesser-of protection attaching automatically;\n\n**(3)** each individual enrolled in qualifying employer coverage holds **dormant** coverage under \u00a7 20-2(b); and\n\n**(4)** every other eligible individual holds coverage in the \u00a7 20-2 default. **An individual missed by every record is enrolled at point of care under \u00a7 20-6**."},{"anchor":"sec-24-3","heading":"\u00a7 24-3. The point-of-sale guarantee, and the backstop that is pre-authorized","part":null,"body":"**(a)** During the transition period, a pharmacy or provider unable to verify an individual's coverage or plan **shall rely on the individual's attestation**, is held harmless, and is paid, per \u00a7 20-6 \u2014 with claims reconciled among payers afterward and **never against the individual or the dispenser.**\n\n**(b) THE STATE BACKSTOP, PRE-AUTHORIZED.** \u2014 A State may elect to operate an emergency point-of-sale backstop during the backstop period under subsection (c-1)(5) \u2014 paying claims that fail to process and being **reimbursed by the United States at cost, automatically, on submission of records, without negotiation, agreement, or discretion.** A collection of information necessary to a payment or reimbursement required by this section is deemed to have been reviewed and approved for purposes of subchapter I of chapter 35 of title 44, United States Code, and no control number under section 3507(a)(3) of that title is a condition of the collection or of the payment; section 3507(a)(1) of that title does not apply to the collection, and the approval so deemed does not expire under section 3507(g) of that title.\n\n**(c) MONEY, AND THE PEOPLE WHO ARE PAID FROM IT.** \u2014 There are appropriated to the Center, out of any money in the Treasury not otherwise appropriated, such sums as are necessary to carry out this section, including the compensation of the officers and employees of the United States who perform its functions, to remain available until expended and without fiscal year limitation. The obligation of the United States to pay each amount due to a pharmacy or provider under subsection (a), and each reimbursement under subsection (b), arises by operation of this section, is not limited to or conditioned on the availability of appropriations, and is not suspended by a lapse in appropriations, a failure to apportion or allot, a deferral, or a proposed rescission; an amount under subsection (a) is due not later than the period set in the schedule after the claim for it is received, a claim being received on the earlier of the date shown by the records of the Center or its contractor and the date shown by the pharmacy's or provider's transaction record, which is conclusive of that date except on fraud, and an amount not paid when due under subsection (a) bears interest from the date due until paid at the rate that applies under chapter 39 of title 31, United States Code, to a late payment to a business concern; an amount unpaid when due under subsection (a) or subsection (c-1)(3), with the interest that subsection or paragraph provides, is recoverable as a claim founded on an Act of Congress. The officers and employees of the United States who perform the payment and the reconciliation among payers under subsection (a), and the reimbursement under subsection (b) and subsection (c-1), are exempt from furlough during a lapse in appropriations and are paid from that appropriation on the ordinary schedule; section 1341(c)(2) of title 31, United States Code, does not delay their pay, and applies to each of them, as though the officer or employee were an excepted employee within the meaning of that section and without regard to any definition of the Office of Personnel Management, only to secure pay not received on the ordinary schedule. In this subsection, an officer or employee is \"exempt\" if the officer or employee continues in duty status during a lapse in appropriations and is paid on the ordinary schedule; no agency may define the term otherwise. Their status under this subsection does not depend on any definition, guidance, or designation of the Office of Personnel Management or of the Office of Management and Budget. The Secretary and the Administrator shall each identify those officers and employees as exempt in any plan prepared or published by or for the Department of Health and Human Services or the Center for a lapse in appropriations, whether or not any such plan is required or published; the absence of a plan does not affect their status under this subsection. A lapse in appropriations, a failure to apportion, and a failure to allot suspend no function or duty under this section, other than the making of a payment that section 1517(a) of title 31, United States Code, forbids, and no officer's or employee's status under this subsection; an amount that falls due under subsection (a) or subsection (c-1)(3) while any of them continues remains due and bears interest as that subsection or paragraph provides. To the extent a function of this section is not financed by that appropriation, the employment of personal services for it is authorized by law for purposes of section 1342 of title 31, United States Code, and an obligation incurred for it before an appropriation is made is authorized by law for purposes of section 1341(a)(1)(B) of that title.\n\n**(c-1) PAYMENT OF THE BACKSTOP.** \u2014\n\n**(1) WHO PAYS.** \u2014 The Administrator shall pay each reimbursement under subsection (b), by electronic funds transfer through the system by which the Center made payments to that State under title XIX on the day before the transition date, or its successor.\n\n**(2) THE RECORDS.** \u2014 The records under subsection (b) are, for each claim the State paid, the date of service, an identifier of the individual, the dispenser or provider, the item or service, the amount the State paid, and the rejection, or the absence of a response, from the payer of record at the time of service, in the standard claim format the State used for such claims on the day before the transition date. The Administrator may publish a format; a submission containing those elements in the State's own format satisfies this paragraph whether or not a format is published, and no other record may be required as a condition of payment. A reimbursement computed from records meeting this paragraph is a legal obligation under the appropriation involved for purposes of section 3528 of title 31, United States Code.\n\n**(3) WHEN DUE.** \u2014 Not later than the period set in the schedule after a submission, the Administrator shall pay 95 percent of the amount claimed, and not later than the second period set in the schedule after the submission shall pay the remainder, less any amount the records do not support. Where the amounts paid under this paragraph for a submission exceed the amount the records support, the excess is set off against later reimbursements to that State, and to the extent none remains due, is recoverable from the State. An amount not paid when due under this paragraph is due whether or not the submission has been reviewed, and bears interest from the date due until paid at the rate that applies under chapter 39 of title 31, United States Code, to a late payment to a business concern.\n\n**(4) WHAT FAILED TO PROCESS.** \u2014 A claim failed to process if the payer of record rejected it, or did not respond, at the time of service, and the dispenser's or provider's transaction record is conclusive of that fact except on fraud. No reimbursement may be reduced or denied because the claim could have been paid under subsection (a) or \u00a7 20-6, or because the individual's coverage was not reflected in the Center's records. An amount owed by a payer of record for a reimbursed claim is recovered by the Administrator from that payer, by set-off against any payment the program owes it and otherwise as a claim of the United States, never from the State, the dispenser, or the individual.\n\n**(5) THE PERIOD.** \u2014 The backstop period under subsection (b) is the period set in the schedule beginning on the transition date, and reimbursement runs from the first claim a State pays in it. A State may submit records for a claim paid in that period until the end of the further period set in the schedule after it ends."},{"anchor":"sec-24-4","heading":"\u00a7 24-4. Contracts, novation, and the workforce","part":null,"body":"**(a) STATES AS PAID CONTRACTORS.** \u2014 The Center may contract with any State, at negotiated cost-based rates, for transition services: records conversion, eligibility-workforce continuity, LTSS operational continuity, and local presence. **Contracting is voluntary on both sides, creates no duty by its absence (\u00a7 21-1A), and its absence is planned for** \u2014 subsection (d).\n\n**(b) MCO NOVATION.** \u2014 A managed-care contract under the former titles is, at the plan's election, novated to a transition-administration contract \u2014 claims processing and care-management continuity for its former enrollment, at administrative cost, through the transition period; plan conversion into the Public Option Act's chassis proceeds on that vehicle's terms.\n\n**(c) THE EXPERIENCED WORKFORCE.** \u2014 The Center shall extend priority hiring to employees of State agencies administering the former titles.\n\n**(d) NO STATE'S ABSENCE BREAKS ANYTHING.** \u2014 Where a State declines to contract, the Center performs with federal machinery and the \u00a7 20-6/\u00a7 24-3 fallbacks; **records held by a State are obtained on payment of the reasonable cost of production, and the absence of State records shifts the classification default toward active coverage, never away from coverage.**"},{"anchor":"sec-24-5","heading":"\u00a7 24-5. Person-level continuity \u2014 money, care, and paper","part":null,"body":"**(a) MONEY.** \u2014 \u00a7 17-2(e): no individual pays more at any time than pre-transition law would have charged.\n\n**(b) CARE.** \u2014 An ongoing course of treatment continues with the treating provider, on the pre-transition terms, for not less than the period set in the schedule \u2014 and for pregnancy, through postpartum completion; for active cancer treatment, through the course. **No network, plan, or payment conversion interrupts a course of treatment.**\n\n**(c) PAPER.** \u2014 A prior authorization, referral, or plan-of-care approval valid the day before the transition date **remains valid for its term or 180 days, whichever is longer.**"},{"anchor":"sec-24-6","heading":"\u00a7 24-6. Nothing moves by reason of the transition itself","part":null,"body":"**(a)** In every payment model, benchmark, attribution, quality measure, and risk arrangement continuing under this title, the Center shall hold constant the effect of the transition \u2014 population, payment-rule, and coding effects alike \u2014 so that **no participant gains or loses by reason of the transition itself**, and shall publish each adjustment with the data sufficient to reconstruct it. \n\n**(b)** The \u00a7 21-1(c) continuation, \u00a7 21-3(a) deeming, and this section are construed together: **the transition changes who administers and who pays \u2014 it changes no one's entitlement, except as \u00a7 21-1(c) provides, no one's payment for the same thing, except as \u00a7 21-1(c) provides, and no one's measured performance.**"},{"anchor":"sec-24-7","heading":"\u00a7 24-7. Publication","part":null,"body":"**(a) WHAT IS PUBLISHED.** \u2014 The Center publishes, monthly during the transition period, and **annually thereafter without limitation of time**, each monthly publication not later than the period set in the schedule after the close of the month it covers and each annual publication not later than the period set in the schedule after the close of the fiscal year it covers, the month or fiscal year covered being the publication period \u2014\n\n**(1)** enrollment in each class described in paragraphs (1) through (4) of \u00a7 24-2, counted as the individuals the Center's records place in the class, with the source of those records and the date through which they are complete stated in each publication;\n\n**(2)** the number of claims paid, and the amounts paid, in reliance on an attestation under \u00a7 20-6 or \u00a7 24-3(a) and under the backstop in \u00a7 24-3(b);\n\n**(3)** the time from receipt of a claim to its payment or denial, at the median and at the ninetieth percentile, stated separately for claims submitted in reliance on an attestation under \u00a7 20-6(a) or \u00a7 24-3(a), and the same measures, stated for each private plan option under the Public Option Act, for the claims for items and services furnished to its enrollees, as reported under subsection (e), each stated separately for claims paid and claims denied, with the share denied and the share of those later paid;\n\n**(4)** the roster of contracts under \u00a7 24-4(a) and novations under \u00a7 24-4(b); and\n\n**(5)** **the time from the date on which an individual made an attestation of a decrease in household income under \u00a7 17-3(c), being the earlier of the dates shown by the attestation and by the record of the channel through which it was made, to the date on which the last of the records from which the Center computes the individual's contribution and cost sharing applies the attested household income, and the time from the date of service on the first claim received in reliance on an attestation under \u00a7 20-6(a), or relied on under \u00a7 24-3(a) where the individual's coverage was not reflected in the records available to the provider, for an individual to the date on which the records from which the Center answers a provider's inquiry about coverage first reflect that individual's coverage or a subsequent determination under \u00a7 20-6(b)**, each at the median and at the ninetieth percentile, computed over the cases in which the later event occurred in the publication period, and separately, as time elapsed at the close of the publication period, over the cases in which it had not occurred by then, and the number of cases in each set, and, for the second interval, separately for cases in which the records first reflected the individual's coverage and cases in which they first reflected a determination under \u00a7 20-6(b) that the individual is not covered, with the number of each. In this paragraph, the date on which records first apply or first reflect a fact is the date on which the entry is made, whatever date from which the entry gives the fact effect; and an attestation is each statement of current household income that the Center's records show was received from the individual or from a person acting for the individual, other than a determination under \u00a7 17-3(a) or return information under \u00a7 17-3(f), and that states household income lower than the household income those records then apply, one the Center does not apply being pending. The Center, and each contractor performing for it a function timed under this paragraph, records for each case each date this paragraph times and the date on which each entry it times was made, and retains the record for not less than six years after the publication in which the case is first counted. The records of such a contractor are records of the Center for this section. In this section, a contractor includes a subcontractor at any tier, and a function a subcontractor performs is performed for the Center. A case for which neither the attestation nor the record of the channel shows the date on which the attestation was made is counted as having taken longer than the period set in the schedule for its interval under subsection (c). A case for which the records do not show the date on which an entry was made is counted, in the first publication made after the Center's records show the entry, as having taken longer than the period set in the schedule for its interval under subsection (c), and, in the median and the ninetieth percentile, as having taken the longer of that period and the time from the start of its interval to the close of the publication period in which the Center's records first show the entry.\n\n**(b) WHAT IS COUNTED, ANNUALLY.** \u2014 The annual publication also states the number of attestations of a decrease under \u00a7 17-3(c) for which household income as later determined at reconciliation under \u00a7 17-3(d) exceeded the household income attested, and, of those, the number made by an individual for whom an attestation was also so exceeded for the preceding period, a count under this subsection being no finding that any attestation was false; the amount by which the assistance provided on those attestations exceeded the assistance that household income as later determined would have yielded, none of which is recoverable by reason of \u00a7 17-3(d); the number of changes of option restored under \u00a7 20-3(c) on the individual's statement that the change was not the individual's election, stated for each plan to which a restored change had been made; and the number of declinations under \u00a7 20-4 revoked in the year, stated by the time each declination was in effect, and the number of those revocations made on a date on which the individual received an item or service for which a claim was paid. The count of claims paid in reliance on an attestation under \u00a7 20-6(a), or under \u00a7 24-3(a) where the individual's coverage was not reflected in the records available to the provider, by provider, is the publication under \u00a7 20-6(c).\n\n**(c) A SHORTFALL, AND THE STAFF THAT WOULD CLOSE IT.** \u2014 Where, in any publication, the share of the cases timed under subsection (a)(5) for either interval that took longer than the period set in the schedule for that interval, or were pending longer than that period at the close of the publication period, exceeds the share set in the schedule for that interval, the publication states that share together with the number of full-time-equivalent personnel performing the function timed in the publication period and the number of full-time-equivalent personnel performing it, stated separately for employees of the Center and for the personnel of each contractor performing it for the Center, that, at the average number of cases completed per full-time equivalent of each over the preceding twelve months or, where the function has been performed for fewer than twelve months, over the months in which it has been performed, would complete within the period set in the schedule the cases received at the rate of the publication period, and, separately, the number of full-time-equivalent months that would complete each case then pending longer than that period, with the method of the computation, including the basis on which personnel are attributed to the function, and the data sufficient to reconstruct it; where no personnel are attributed to the function, the publication states the cases pending longer than the period and the process or system in which each was pending. Each contract under which a contractor performs a function timed under subsection (a)(5), whatever the date on which it was entered into, requires the contractor to keep and retain the records subsection (a)(5) describes and to report to the Center, for each month, the full-time-equivalent personnel performing it and the cases it completed; a contract in force when this subsection takes effect includes the term from that date by operation of this subsection. For a contractor that has not reported for a month, its cases completed per full-time equivalent for that month are taken to be the lowest of the average for employees of the Center, the average of each contractor that reported for that month, and each number the contractor itself reported for any of the preceding twelve months, and its full-time-equivalent personnel performing the function to be the cases the Center's records show it completed divided by that number; where no such number exists, the publication states, for that contractor, the cases pending longer than the period and the process or system in which each was pending. The publication names each contractor to which this sentence applied and each month for which it applied. The Administrator transmits the statement to the Committee on Finance and the Committee on Appropriations of the Senate and to the Committee on Ways and Means, the Committee on Energy and Commerce, and the Committee on Appropriations of the House of Representatives, with each publication in which the condition in this subsection is satisfied. A period or share set in the schedule for this subsection is a standard of measurement for this section only, and does not delay, qualify, or excuse the immediate effect of an attestation under \u00a7 17-3(c) or the enrollment in the records under \u00a7 20-6(a).\n\n**(d)** Publication under this section, and transmission under subsection (c), are ministerial. A collection of information necessary to a publication required by this section is deemed to have been reviewed and approved for purposes of subchapter I of chapter 35 of title 44, United States Code, and no control number under section 3507(a)(3) of that title is a condition of the collection or of the publication; section 3507(a)(1) of that title does not apply to the collection, and the approval so deemed does not expire under section 3507(g) of that title.\n\n**(e) PLAN CLAIMS, TIMED.** \u2014 A private plan option under the Public Option Act shall report to the Secretary, in the form and at the intervals the Public Option Act provides, the date on which it received, and the date on which it paid or denied, each claim for an item or service furnished to an enrollee, and a report so made satisfies this subsection. Until the Public Option Act provides them, the report is made for each calendar month, not later than the period set in the schedule after its close, and a report stating, for every claim received by the close of the month and not shown as decided in an earlier report, the enrollee and claim identifiers, the provider, the date of service, the date of receipt, and any date of payment or denial and which it was, satisfies this subsection whether or not a form is published; a claim reported without a date of payment or denial is pending. A report is made on the earlier of the dates shown by the Secretary's records and by the plan's transmission record, which is conclusive of that date except on fraud. A publication under subsection (a)(3) names each plan that has not reported for its publication period. For the Center's measures and each plan's under subsection (a)(3), a claim is received on the earliest date on which it reached the Center, the plan, or any person acting for either, whether or not it was returned for correction; a claim for an item or service whose earlier claim was denied is received when the earlier one was; and a claim denied and later paid is timed to its payment. A claim shown by an encounter record under section 18-1(d) of the Public Option Act for which the plan has not reported a date is counted as pending at the close of the publication period, and the Center's measures and each plan's include the claims pending, as time elapsed, with their number."},{"anchor":"schedule","heading":"SCHEDULE","part":null,"body":"*(Reserved.)*\n\n**\u00a7 17 values:** 1. **The floor** (\u00a7 17-1(a-1)): [___]% of the poverty level (working assumption: 150 percent)\n2. **Cost-sharing phase band** (\u00a7 17-1(f)): from the floor to [___]% of the poverty level\n3. **OOP-limit band and curve** (\u00a7 17-1(g)): limits from $[___] to $[___] across incomes [___]% to [___]% of poverty\n4. **Deepening trigger share** (\u00a7 17-4(a)): cost sharing exceeding [___]% of household income\n5. **Reconciliation period** (\u00a7 17-3): [___] (working assumption: the plan year)\n6. **Contribution curve** (\u00a7 17-1(a)): the required percentage of household income at each level of household income from the floor to the income at which the contribution reaches its maximum: [points ___] \u2014 POLICY-VISIBLE\n7. **Comparison statement** (\u00a7 17-2(e-1)): [___] days after the close of the reconciliation period \u2014 COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL\n8. **Plan cost-sharing report, until the Public Option Act provides one** (\u00a7 17-4(d)): [___] days after the close of the month \u2014 COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL\n\n**\u00a7 20 values:** 1. **Annual election period** (\u00a7 20-3(b)): [___] weeks, ending [___]\n2. **Qualifying changes in circumstances** (\u00a7 20-3(b)): [enumerated list for the committee \u2014 loss of coverage, move, household change]\n3. **Reliance-review margin** (\u00a7 20-6(c)): [___] percentage points above the share among providers of the same type or specialty in the same geographic unit \u2014 COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL\n4. **Reliance-review geographic unit** (\u00a7 20-6(c)): [___] \u2014 COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL\n5. **Change-of-option notice** (\u00a7 20-3(c)): [___] days after the change \u2014 COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL\n6. **Report of a plan's or contractor's disclosure** (\u00a7 20-2(f)): [___] days after the disclosure or, where a court order forbids revealing it, after the order expires \u2014 COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL\n7. **Completion of the status check** (\u00a7 20-1(a-3)(3)): [___] days after the request, excluding any period to respond \u2014 COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL\n8. **Notice in the year a child turns 18** (\u00a7 20-1(a-4)): [___] days before the annual election period begins, and not less than the period in value 7 before January 1 \u2014 COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL\n9. **Transmission of a request made to a provider or contractor** (\u00a7 20-1(a-3)(1)): [___] days \u2014 COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL\n10. **Response by the Commissioner of Social Security or the Secretary of Homeland Security** (\u00a7 20-1(a-3)(2)): [___] days after receipt \u2014 COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL\n11. **Federal enrollment records to the Center** (\u00a7 21-4(e)(4)): [___] days before the transition date \u2014 COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL\n12. **Notice before a year of temporary lawful presence ends** (\u00a7 20-1(a-3)(6)(C)): [___] days before the last month of the period, and not less than the period in value 7 \u2014 COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL\n13. **Assistance for the months a status check was pending** (\u00a7 20-1(a-3)(4), (6)(D)(iii)): [___] days after the check establishes status or the notice under \u00a7 20-1(a-3)(6)(D)(i) is received \u2014 COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL\n14. **Notice by the Secretary of Homeland Security of an extension of a temporary status** (\u00a7 20-1(a-3)(6)(D)(i)): [___] days after the grant, or after a notice or regulation extending the status for a class takes effect \u2014 COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL\n15. **Notice after a temporary status ends with no notice of extension** (\u00a7 20-1(a-3)(6)(D)): [___] days after the last day of the status \u2014 COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL\n\n**\u00a7 21 values:** 1. **Continuation period** (\u00a7 21-1(c)(1)): through the last day of the first fiscal year of the State concerned, as established, on the date of enactment of this Act, by the law governing that State's fiscal year, that begins on or after the transition date \u2014 POLICY-VISIBLE\n2. **Publication of continued functions** (\u00a7 21-1(c)(6)): [___] days after the transition date \u2014 COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL\n3. **Coverage-return disclosure** (\u00a7 21-4(h-1); 26 U.S.C. 6103(l)(24)(A)): [120] days after the latest of the filing date, receipt, and a required matching agreement taking effect \u2014 VERIFIED (6103(l)(12)(D)(ii): \"120 days after the date the request was made\", re-anchored)\n4. **Matching agreement in effect** (\u00a7 21-4(h-1)(4)): [___] days after the date of enactment \u2014 COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL\n5. **Request for return information** (\u00a7 21-4(f)(2)): [___] days after an individual is first described, and [___] days after the individual income tax filing date in each later year \u2014 COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL\n6. **Return-information disclosure** (\u00a7 21-4(f); 26 U.S.C. 6103(l)(23)(A)): [120] days after the later of the request and a required matching agreement taking effect \u2014 VERIFIED (6103(l)(12)(D)(ii): \"120 days after the date the request was made\")\n\n**\u00a7 22 values:** 1. **Election notice period** (\u00a7 22-2(e)): [___] months\n2. **Demonstration maximum term** (\u00a7 22-3(b)(4)): [___] years\n3. **Center-funded demonstration budget** (\u00a7 22-3(b)(5)): $[___] per year in aggregate\n4. **Price of a purchase of withheld assistance** (\u00a7 22-2(c)): [___] days after the Center receives the State's law \u2014 COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL\n\n**\u00a7 24 values:** 1. Transition date (\u00a7 24-1): first day of the second year after enactment\n2. Backstop period (\u00a7 24-3(c-1)(5)): 180 days, beginning on the transition date\n3. Continuity-of-care period (\u00a7 24-5(b)): [___] months\n4. Authorization carryover (\u00a7 24-5(c)): 180 days floor\n5. Transition period (\u00a7 24-1(c)): [___] months\n6. Interim reimbursement (\u00a7 24-3(c-1)(3)): [___] days after submission \u2014 COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL\n7. Final reimbursement (\u00a7 24-3(c-1)(3)): [___] days after submission \u2014 COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL\n8. Submission after the backstop period (\u00a7 24-3(c-1)(5)): [___] months \u2014 COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL\n9. **Attestation-to-effect interval** (\u00a7 24-7(c); \u00a7 17-3(c)): a period of [___] days, and a share exceeding it of [___] percent constituting a shortfall \u2014 COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL\n10. **Point-of-care record-entry interval** (\u00a7 24-7(c); \u00a7 20-6(a)): a period of [___] days, and a share exceeding it of [___] percent constituting a shortfall \u2014 COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL\n11. Monthly publication (\u00a7 24-7(a)): [___] days after the close of the month covered \u2014 COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL\n12. Annual publication (\u00a7 24-7(a)): [___] days after the close of the fiscal year covered \u2014 COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL\n13. **Payment under the point-of-sale guarantee** (\u00a7 24-3(a), (c)): [___] days after receipt of the claim \u2014 COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL\n14. **Plan claim-dates report, until the Public Option Act provides one** (\u00a7 24-7(e)): [___] days after the close of the month \u2014 COMMITTEE-TECHNICAL"},{"anchor":"sec-0-6","heading":"\u00a7 0-6. Definitions, and references to sections of related Acts","part":null,"body":"**(a) SCOPE.** \u2014 A reference in this Act to a section not contained in this Act is a reference to that section of the Medicare Covers Teeth, Eyes, and Ears Act, the Long-Term Care Act, the Public Option Act, the Lowest Drug Price Wins Act, the Medical Care PAID Act, the Keeping Hospitals Open Act, the Patient Safety and Compensation Act, the Ending Surprise Ambulance Bills and Fake ERs Act, and the Keep Your Benefits Act, as the context requires.\n\n**(b) IF THE RELATED ACT IS NOT IN EFFECT.** \u2014 Where a section referred to under subsection (a) is not in effect, the reference applies as that section read on the date of enactment of this Act.\n\n**(c) IF THE SECTION HAS NEVER TAKEN EFFECT.** \u2014 Where a provision of this Act conditions a consequence on a report, program, payment, or determination established by a section not contained in this Act, and that section has never taken effect, **the condition is inapplicable and the remainder of the provision operates according to its terms.**\n\n**(d) THE PROGRAM.** \u2014 In this Act and in each related Act, \"the program\" means coverage and benefits under this Act.\n\n**(e) STATE.** \u2014 In this Act, \"State\" means each of the several States, the District of Columbia, the Commonwealth of Puerto Rico, the United States Virgin Islands, Guam, the Commonwealth of the Northern Mariana Islands, and American Samoa.\n\n**(f) DATE OF INTRODUCTION.** \u2014 In this Act, \"the date of introduction of this Act\" means the date on which the bill enacted as this Act was introduced in its House of origin.\n\n**(g) A COURSE OF TREATMENT BEGUN.** \u2014 For purposes of \u00a7 21-1(c)(5), \u00a7 22-2(e), and \u00a7 24-5(b), a course of treatment has begun before a date where an item or service in the course was furnished to the individual before that date, as shown by a claim or encounter record under title XIX or XXI of the Social Security Act, under this title, or under a function continued by \u00a7 21-1(c) or an election under \u00a7 22-2; a course ongoing on the transition date, for \u00a7 24-5(b), is one so begun before that date. No determination by any officer is required.\n\n**(h) TO WHOM A DUTY TO PUBLISH OR FURNISH IS OWED.** \u2014 Each publication this Act requires the Center or its Administrator to make, and each statement or notice this Act requires either to furnish to an individual, including under \u00a7 17-2(e-1), \u00a7 20-2(f), \u00a7 20-3(c), \u00a7 20-6(c), \u00a7 21-1(c)(6), \u00a7 22-2(c) and (f), \u00a7 22-3(c), \u00a7 24-6(a), and \u00a7 24-7, is a ministerial duty. A publication is owed to the public, to each individual whose coverage or assistance the published measures describe, and to every person whom its nonperformance injures; a statement or notice is owed to the individual to whom it is to be furnished and to every person whom its nonperformance injures; each so that the adequacy of the program's administration can be known and corrected, and so that each individual can know, and seek correction of, the coverage, assistance, and amounts the program applies to the individual."},{"anchor":"sec-0-7","heading":"\u00a7 0-7. Severability","part":null,"body":"**(a)** If any provision of this Act, or the application of any provision to any person or circumstance, is held invalid, the remainder of this Act and the application of the provision to any other person or circumstance are not affected.\n\n**(b) NOT ONE INTEGRATED SCHEME.** \u2014 The provisions of this Act are separate and independent. **A court shall not treat this Act as a single integrated scheme, and shall not infer from the invalidity of one provision that the Congress would not have enacted the remainder.**"},{"anchor":"sec-0-8","heading":"\u00a7 0-8. Effective dates","part":null,"body":"**(a) IN GENERAL.** \u2014 Except as otherwise provided, this Act takes effect on the first day of the first plan year beginning after the date that is one year after the date of enactment.\n\n**(b) SCHEDULE VALUES.** \u2014 Where a provision refers to an amount, period, threshold, or rate set in the schedule, that value is the value stated in the SCHEDULE to this Act."}],"asOf":"2026-11-18"}}}};
